      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <p>4") - “)fl 141 ILS INE Ni op() U., (-4 AL L'S SELF-RAISING FI.OTTP :</p>
        <p>. , i;AVi 1 Ai</p>
        <p>Xi%</p>
        <p>Mr. Chamberlain's selection of members for the Sugar Industriea Commissions has given Much satisfaction, and the commission is generally re- garded as a strong and competent one. The presence • of Sir Edward Grey upon it is' regarded ii Liberal circles not merely as a well deserved com- pliment to a young and rising statesman, but as a pleig, that Mr. Chamberlain has been anxious to place the inquiry in the hands of trusted and competent men. Sir D. Barbour also commands much confidence. Be has earned his laurels as ,a financier i 4 other fields, and his past career is a pledge that the inquiry will be thorough. Sir Henry Norman, though really a man of a brilliant Indian career, has been Governoi of Jamaica, and therefore eaters upon the investigfi- tion with a wide knowledga' of the question These will be aided by Colonial Office experts, and the two groups should surely get at the bottom of the problem.</p>
        <p>The proposal to draw upon the coasting popula- tion of the colonies for recruits for the Royal Navy has provoked a geed deal of outside enthusiasm, but • I am told 'on good authority that in officio; quarters. it.tas not been received with anything like! the same warmth. The. question has been again and again discussed by the naval experts,- and they evidently recognise that there are many difficulties in the way. . To begin with, they are by no means satisfied that there is' anything like the abundance of good material to draw -.upon which has been represented as existing. Even in regard to the much quoted case of the Newfoundland seafaring population they are at least a little sceptical.as tD whether it will yield the kind of men Iv ho are really required in the navy, and one-well-known naval ' expert. went so far us to say a day or two ago that 'Ne weuld rather have one east coast -fisherman than- nalf-a-doien Newfoundlanders. As .to the other colonies, the opinion seems to be that the men who would volunteer for the navy would be very far [rota t;eing th salt of the population, while mere harbour loafer is not the ki4tl of man to•• turn into&amp; sma.,-t British tar.</p>
        <p>The question is repeatedly asked : Why has not a I fund been opened at the Mansion House for the 1 ttimitie;-stricken, people of India when the distress I,i undoubtedly acute in cone districts and half a million ,people are employed on relief works? We' nndentand the Lord, Mayor is anxious to open a ;und,,and has communicated with the Government -everal times on the subject,bnt ri'l) decision has yet been arrived at. The Lord Mayor has received i,hotographs of natives, manY of them mere skele- tons, and the case is undoribte,ily urgent; but he points out. that he can do nothing without tio-i con- sent of the authdrities, who are directly respon- sibl. If the appeal for funds is delaNed until some great mortality occurs among:l'le famine-stricken the Government will undoubtedly incur A grav,-.? responsibility. When the last great. famine itnate,..; Southern India more than a•half million t,terl;ng was contributed to the Mansion House Re- lict Fund.</p>
        <p>One of the interesting survivals of mediteval times wag to,,be witneised on Monday in the elec- tions of Common.Councillors to the old unreformed Corporation of the City of London.lt is sixty years since the I\lunicipal CorPorotions Act put !he aol erntuent of every other city in the country on r, really representative and fairly democratic basis, with guarantees for honest administration and rational development. But the London Corpora- tion escaped reform then, and successfully resists it still, and the little cliques of stew individuals in the streets which cluster round St. Paul's and form I'mt, a sp4lck on the map of real London still go on inesqueradinF as the municipality of the metrOpo- Vs. •</p>
        <p>• The Liberals of the Cleveland Division will.lupie a heavy account to settle With their fellow.• Liberals el,ewhera if they fail to carry the day in the com- ing election. They are already exposed to severe criticism hera for handicapping themselves with a candidal-e who cannot take part in the coiatest. In recent times so much importance has 'been attached to ,the result of by-elections as an indication of the feeling of the country that it would hardly be 'lin exaggeration to say that bye-lections have almost ns profoundly modified the policy of parties and the legislbition of Parliament as a general election it,elf. Under these circurnstances, it IA little .leSs hen treason to the party for the leaders inithe cot- sti tuency which for the moment is promoted to thi- ,uureme honour of holding the pass to throw awa their chances of success.</p>
        <p>Lord galisbury's action in summoning the Am -bassadors to the Foreign Office on Monday instead of Wednesday thil week was not due, as the semi- effioial explanation would have us believe, to the Christmas party which was to be held at Itatfield hut -to the receipt. of a definite proposal for joint. intervention by the Powers in the administra- tion of Turkey, made by the Russian Government.. This proposal was received by Lord Salisbury at the end of last week, and it was to discuss it: that the Prime Minister summoned Sir Michael Hicks-Beach in London,in order that he might have the assist- !owe of his counsel es well as that of the .Nlinisters in town. The purpose of summoning the-Ambassa- dors was to communicate to them the views of her Majwsty's Government on the proposals of the Russian Governrnent,in order that each Ambassador might communicate with his own Government, and t hus enable the rePre.entativea of the Great Powers at Constsntple to be ,properly instructed. In ffect,therefore, the Concert of the Powers is being maintained, but in every fresh move Russia is pow taking the lead. I have reason to believe that un- less Turkey shows within the naxt ten days a. defer- ence of which she is at present little suspected some very decisive steps will be undertaken in the name of the joint Powers.</p>
        <p>Dr. Temple was not allowed to•day to enter into tull possession of the See of Canterbury without a protest. Curiously enough, the ground of objec- tion was that the Archbishop-elect was a self-con- fessed believer in the full doctrine of evolution. The solitary objector, who put forward a protest founded on inch a ground, did not seem to have any respect for the modern tendency of the broadEr ecclesiastical spirit, which seeks to bring even evo- lution into harmony with Christian truth. If the roue which had been empowered to confirm the election had entertained the objection, Dr. Temple would not have been in possession of the See of Canterbury for several months. An inquiry into the theological bearings of evolution in particular, on Scriptural doctrine, would have been one of the keenkst sensetions of the time for all the enemies of Chri-tianity, and that it lay beyond the power of the court to listen to an objection of this kind wax fortunately in accordance both with ecclesiastical law and with reason. At the same time, One can- not but admire the courage of the solitary protester, who stack to his point with a spirit worthy of a martyr.</p>
        <p>• Two Slarylehone magistratesi have brought cab law into a curious predicament by taking diame- trically opposite views of the same point. Con- sequently, if a case of plying for hire comes before ene magistrate the cabmen will be the victors, but if it comes before the other magistrate the railway companies will win. It is a most absurd dilemma. The contradiction relates to the Conapanies, in their dispute with the privileged cabmen, carrying pas- sengers in an omnibus driven by an unlicensed driver. One of the magistrates refused to convict in one Case, while the other convicted in another case a day or two afterwards. Some hieer court would be required to set one of these gentlemen right, --- ?mow As the ote,come of some new arrangements which have now been satisfactorily concluded, the liational Liberal Club will in future be managed by a joint committee of the club and of the buildings companv—consisting of members of the club—who own the premises in which the club is housed. It is understood to be one condition of the new 41e- , parture that the club will henceforth take a more active part in the political work of the—Liberal party, and that the library will be separately vested lin trustees as a permanent memorial of Mr. Glad- stone.</p>
