      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>SUNDERED REMITS;</head>
        <p>THE SUNDERED REMITS;</p>
        <p>WAR OF THE HOUSEHOLD. Author of "The Double Life," "The Bailiff's Scheme," &amp;c., &amp;c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>By the</head>
        <p>By the</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXXVII.</p>
        <p>Whilst these events, which so closely concerned Dora, welt transpiring, Dora herself was rapidly becoming acquainted with her new protectress, and was beginning to feel at home among her new surroundings. Soon after the establishment of a full under- standing between them, tiny Mrs. Peyser, with a , motherly look that sat oddly on her small withered face escorted Dora to a pretty room up stairs looking toward the river, and left her there to sleep off her fatigues. It was past noon when the girl awoke. She sprang up with a start, and with the expec- tation of finding herself back in her prison chamber at the Black Cottage, at Chiswick; but her bright and pleasant surroundings immediately reassured her, and brought a delicious sense of peace to her soul. _</p>
        <p>"I should like to stay here forever," she thought, looking wistfully around then sunny room, with its snow-white bed drapery, its simple cane-seat chair, and its cozy chintz-covered couch. "It seems such a safe refuge, after all I 'ye been through." She hastened to make her toilet. It was simple enough, for she had no change of garments beyond a fresh collar and cuffs. These were donned, her dress carefully dusted, her hair brushed into a thousand bewitching rings, curling close to her noble little bead, and Dora then began to think of rejoining her hostess.</p>
        <p>She was still thinking of it when a low knock sounded on the door, and the diminutive hostess— looking like a little old doll in a trailing dinner dresa—oPened the door and camein. "I heard you up, my dear," she said, e4lain- iugly, "and so came up for you. Did you rest well ?" _</p>
        <p>"Very well," said Dora, gratefully. "I was very tired, and I almost wondered that I waked up at all. Is Noel--- Squire Weir still here ?" A flood of blusheQ swept over her lac:: ia tides as she asked the question. "No, Thy fiear," responded the old lady, well pleased at Dora's display of colour. "I sent him away long ago. IVA well to have him watching those NurrE. There's no knowing what they will do next." -</p>
        <p>" Rowe much trouble I am to Noel !" murmured Dora, scgretfully. "There:are two kinds of trouble," observed the tiny old- holy, sagely ; "an unwelcome sort, that we have to bear, and a plessant sort of trouble, that we take on ourselves in orler to make some- body else's 11;:rcit.qis lii;liter. ioy opinion,. my - dear, that Noel likes to taks `eit.re of yOu." " He has been kinder to me than any bro:lers" 'said Dora, shyly. "Well he may be," she said. "Don't you know he loves you, tie,tr ? Don't You know that you ' are snore to bin than all the old dwarf aunts in Christendom? his love. for you is of tile . old, euivalrous sort, Dora, and I-know he would gladly die to securo your happiness. We don't see much of-that sort of love, in this work-a-day world— roueh of the ,elf-saerifiee of which Noel is capable. Did you not kno* he loved you?" There were teats dropping softly from Dora's bright eyes. She could not answer, but she nodded gently. The little old lady looked at her wistfully. It was evident that she wanted to know what Noel's 'chances were of winning this young beauty, with eyes like dusky stars. " I have always known Noel," Dora stammered, after a pause, yielding to the mute appeal of those eyes uplifted to hers. "He was always brave and .honest and good. He was always truthful and, honourable. I don't believe he.oould tell a lie—" "And yet yciu don't love him ?" "Did I say I didn't ?" said Dora, drooping her exquisitely tinted face. . The wee woman beamed all over with smiles. "Come, come_, Dora," she said briskly. "I'm an old goose to be stirring up the sacred emotions of your heart, like a bull flourishing around in a china shop. lam always putting my foot in it, trying to help somebody, but don't yon mind me. What I know I know, and what I guess I can keep to myself. But of one thing you may rest assured, Dora," and her Voice trembled, and she laid her - tiny, shrunken, blue-veined hand on Dora's, "I love you already, for your own-sake as well as Noel's, and I shall be glad to see you Noel's wife. I am not one -of those who -think that to be a lady is only a matter of blood and descent. I have seen vulgar, vixenish women born of rich families, and totally unworthy the name of lady. I have seen true ladies in the lower ranks of life. - gentle, modest, low-voiced women, with kind hearts and 'pleasant ways. Those qualities, I take it, go far toward constituting the true lady. And you, Dora, if 'you,' had -'been the daughter of a scavenger, and what you are, would be none the less a lady." Dora, with a sudden impulse, stooped and kissed the pleasant, earnest face. "And now, let us go down," said her little host- ess; "I want a long talk with you." She opened the door, leading the way down stairs. Dora followed her to a sitting-room look:rig to- ward the river, and directly beneath the chamber assigned the young guest. It was a cozy room—small, as were all the rooms at Holly Cottage. It had a tiny bay-window, that seemed afraid of pushing itself out into notice. The walls were faintly tinted with green, and were adorned with a few crayon sketches and water- colours. A small book-case, a little quaint old cottage piano, open, and with some old-time songs against the sack, a chintz-covered lounge and two great chintz-covered chairs, comprised, the princi- pal features of the furniture. "What a cozy room I" said Dora, admiringly. "It is my sitting-room, and I find it pleasant," returned the gratified hostess, losing herself in the depths of one of the big chairs, while Dora sat down in the other. "I take a great deal of com- fort here, my dear; but of course I have many lonely hours. I should like to keep you here with me all the time, if I could." "And can't you?" asked Dora. • - "How can I, dear, when these Narrs are likely to come and take you from me at any time? rhey May be on your track at this moment. Did any one see you in the course of your journey ?" "We met watermen and others on the river about daybreak, but they didn't speak to us." "But they used their eyes' I'll warrant," said Mrs. Peyser, with a shake of her little grey head. "Did anyone see you land at the foot of my gar- den ?" "No one, unless some people who were in a barge, and who were going toward London." "Hum ! Those barge people may meet the Narrs and be questioned, and they may not. I in- cline to the opinion that the Narrs will think you have, landed near some railway station." • "But they won't find ary abandoned boat ?" "True," said the tiny hostess, rubbing her nose upward in her thoughtfulness. "Well, these Narrs are sure to trace you sooner or later, Dora. Thera is only one safe plan to adopt, only one course to take to keep you out of their clutches forever." "And that ?" asked Dora, breathlessly. "You must marry Noel immediately." "Impossible ,!" murmured the girl., lowering her head. , "By no means. Noel will be back to-morrow. You and he ai's do the prejitninary asking and answering, and Noel shall then procure a special license, arid yau hall be married on the 'day after -to-morrow. Then, when our 'fine Narrs arrive, I will tell them that you have gone on a foreign tour —as you must be—with your husband." The young girl leaned .forward, bending her head out of the open bay-wiudow, and burying her face 'among the fragrant blossoms of. a trumpet honeysuckle, "Dear Mrs. Peyser," she murmured, in a dis- tressed voice, "1 einnot marry Neel—l cannot 1" "And why not ?" asked the wee hostess, rather sharply, in her anxiety for the happiness of Noel. "Because—because he asked me once when I was known as Dora Chessom, the heiress, and I refused him then," stammered poor Dora. "I thought I liked Mr. Warner best " "Indeed 1" said Aunt Tiny, with sarcastic em- phasis, knitting her mat brovve together. "And so you still prefer that perfumed popirjay to my honest, loving Noel? You can't overcome your affection for that worthless object. Well, all I can say is—" She was prevented from uttering her caustic opinion by Dora, who exclaimed, desperately : • - ",You misunderataud me, Mrs.,.Peyser. .I don't like Mr. Warner. I have discovered tne-:, I never loved him. That was only a girlish fu .:ruki I am ashamed of it." "Well, that fellow's conduct hasn't set you against all mankind, has it ?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>SDA 1) F, 6</head>
        <p>SDA 1) F, 6</p>
        <p>At 4,1 P,l!</p>
        <p>Dora shook her head. "Then why won't you marry Noel ?" demanded the sorely puzzled little lady of Holly Cottage. "Because I refused him when I was his social equal," said Dora, half inaudibly, her face quite hidden among the honeysuckle blossoms. "Don't you see, Mrs. Peyser.?" " No—l 'II be whipped if Ido 1" There was a long silence. At last the girl broke it, saying, in a sobbing voice :</p>
        <p>'Couldn't I go somewhere, Mrs. Peyeer, and bide away from all pursuit? I can earn my own living. I have got money. Couldn't I get into somo school or family as a teacher ?" "That Warner will never rest till he finds you. And when he finds you, the Narrs will stand by ready to claim you. You'd better take an old woman's advice, Dora, and marry this young fel- low you despise I" "Oh, no, I don't despise him 1" cried Dora, eag- erly. "He is the noblest, dearest, best—" Her voice broke down.</p>
        <p>The old lady smiled shrewdly. " Well, well," she said, caressingly, "we 'll let the matter rest till to-morrow. Noel shall talk it up with you when he comes 1" And at this point the subject was dropped. Mrs. Peyser took up an embroidery frame as large as herself, and chatted busily while she worked industriously at a group of impossible flowers, which were intended to ornament the centre of a sofa pillow.</p>
        <p>Luncheon wa; eaten. The afternoon wore on, and brought the dinher hour, six o'clock. The ladies adjourned to the dining-room, through whose west window the 'sunlight was stealing in between the interstices of the wanton convolvuli.</p>
        <p>"I always have dinner at.siz," observed Mrs. Peyser,itaking her usual high chair before the meat, and motioning her gut to the opposite cover. "It's true! have. no men abont,. and could as well dine earlier, as solitary women do *gener- ally, but habit is everything.? She carved the meat, and Hannah, the _prira maid, waited upon Dora. Iti• They lingeied nfarlY an hriiu the little room, with its cool st,adows,,,-. and the fragrue exhaling from the drooping hers of the convolvtlii, and then returned to the 'ihitting-room, where they sat and talked until after ;Jun set. "It grows cooler now," 1 ' Mrs. Peyser, throwing open all her shuttt.t- let in the cool evening breeze that came w beginning of the long twilight. " llw look, .Doz•e. ? Your late excitement is not goitg to make you ill?" • . _</p>
        <p>"I think not," sid Dora. " I any. use 4 to the freeh air, and hove had .-)ut little cf it lately since I left the Grange.. flow pleasant it is to see a real country garden! Do you think, Mrs. ,Peyser, that there would be any danger in my running down tO the river and back ag,in Mrs..Peyser mounted a, chair and. looked out. The river stoned silent and deserted. No barges nor boats f-,f any description were visible. No sound of -rough voices- came up from the</p>
        <p>water. "There is nobody out, I think," she said. "It will do you good to take a run down the garden. I would go with you, but a walk at this hour would give me rheurnatils.m enough to last nie a month. I'll call -Hannah." "Please don't," said Dora, coaxingly. "I won't be gone long enough to make' it worth while to call her from her work. I only want a breath of fresh ail', and a bunch of those red roses on the lower trellis by the river wall." Mrs. Peyser smiled assent. Dora ran up stairs for her hat and sample, and a minute later was exploring eagerly the beauties 9f the river•side garden. Little Mrs. Peyser watched her smilingly from the window. The girl tripped along, stooping over the pansy beds, plucking roses, and hurrying down to the river's edge. She paused on the top of the flight of steps, and looked in either direction alternately. There seemed no boat near. There was a strong, fresh breeze blowing down the stream, and the current flowed swiftly seaward. Dora wa4 enjoying the scene, with a blissful sense of security, when a noise at her feet startled her Looking downward she beheld in the shadow of the wall, close up to the stone steps, a sail-boat, the sail lowered. And in this boat crouched her enemies—the Narrs and Warner I For an instant she was stupefieg</p>
        <p>Then she turned to flee. - Too late! Before she had taken two steps, her preAntled parents had leaped out, mounted the stair*, and seized her. "Not a word 1" said Mrs. Narr, fiercely, as the girl str4gled and screamed for help. " Stop that! Who can take you away from your own parents Be silent, or it will be the worse for you 1" Between them, the Narrs seized the girl and bore her down to the boat. Mrs. Narr sat down beside Dora and held her in a close embrace. Warner pushed off, Jack Narr seizing the oats to row the boat midway to the stream. "Pretty cleveily done !" said Warner, as Nan*, dropping the oars, began to hoist the sails. "With this wind and current, we shall be back at Chis- wick bef re we know it. Ah Miss Dora," he added, addressing the despairing young girl, "you little know who you are fighting with! You didn't think we should find you? A bargeman told us your retreat just as we were on the point of landing at Kew. And here you are again, safely in our power." " Which she won't leave again," declared Mrs. Narr, grimly. "I'm tired of this 'ere fifing and blowing. I won't be dancing around for a young girl's whims. You've got to marry Mr. Warner, or I'll starve you into it. You hear that, girl!" "I'll never marry him ," said Dora. "I'll die</p>
        <p>first." - • "We shall have to stupefy her with the drugs," said Narr. "Poor Miss- Dora I'd a helped if. I could. What can she Want better than an honest marriage with the heir to a title ?" .D.)ra.looked up in a great term". They would -drug her into a titiipor and theo c,,rnpr,l her mar- ride to ,ffiding tfll taitt.ph,ut ehetoy. A deaCnly swep:, over her. Aial ttle boat, tiiin her houuded on. in.A. tit !,,i</p>
        <p>EPPS'S COCOA.—GRATEFUL AD COMFORTING.—`4 By a thorough knowledgeiof the natural laws which govern the operat,ons of digestion and nutria°., and by a ..sarelui ap- plication of the fine properties of well-selected Cocas, Mr. Rpps has provided for our breakfast and supper a delic,tely flavoured beverage which may save us many heavy doc- tors' bills it is by the judicious use of such articles of diet that a constitution may be gradually built up until strong enough to resist every teudency to disease. We may escape rr any a f ttal shaft by keeping ourselves well fortified with pure blood and a properly nourished frame."—Civil Service Gazette.- Made simply with boiliEg water or milk.— Sold on y in packets and pound tins, by Grocers, labelled "JAMES RPPS &amp; Co., Ltd. Homceopathic Chemists. London." Also makers of Epps's Ltd., or Cocoa-Nib Extract ; A alio beverage of full flavour, now with many beneficialv taking the place of tea, Its active .orinciple being a gentle nerve stimulant, supplies the needed energy without unduly exciting the system.—(No 2.)</p>
        <p>ASTHMA, CONSUMPTION, COUGHS. loLts, AND BRONCHITIS. RAPIDLY CURED Bi- Da, IA COCK'S PULMONIC WAFERM. Nurse .t4hoesmith. Chilrnark, Salisbiry, writes :—" I don't like being without the Wafers, and only wish every other nursP, as I do, could keep a store in „hand, they would save much suffering in life ; let alone the beon of having a reliable medicine in need. I could send you particulars of cures,&amp;e., &amp;c." They instantly relieae and rapidly cure Asthma, Con- sumption, Bronchitis. Coughs Colds, Shortness of Breath, Phlegm, Paine in the Chest. Rheumatism—and taste plea- santly. Sold at 1.3.10. and2s. 9d. per but.—. Advt. (6)</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXXVIII. ' JOY WITH RITTER IN ESSA W 6114 haltpast eight o'clock, on the long sum- mer evening, when Warne, the Nam; and the captive Dora landed from their beat at Chiswick, and took their way up to the Blaek Cottage in Ivy Lane. . . .</p>
        <p>The old care-taker was on the look out for their coming. The garden door swung open at their approach, and the dame greeted them with noisy rejoicings, being careful, however, to say nothing of her visitors of the afternoon.</p>
        <p>Warner and Nary, with Dora, passed on to the dwelling. Mrs. Narr locked the garden door, and followed the others into the cottage. They were waiting for her in the parlour, where lights were burning. She hastened to bring in a supper of chops and ale. As they ate and drank, Mrs. Narr threatened Dora with all manner of</p>
        <p>penalties unless she yielded to their wishes. "What do you think of that?" asked Felix. "I think," answered Dora, "that you and your allies are well mated."</p>
        <p>Warner's face reddened. "Come, come," he said, authoritatively, "this attitude of yours isn't the proper one. You are totally helpless, and- in our power. But I will make you my honoured wife, Dora. I will give you all the pleasures that life affords. Will you accept them at my hands ?" "They would be purchased at too dear a price," said Dora, coldly. "You loved me once Dora," persisted Warner, sitting down beside her. Is that love all dead? Can no tenderness on my part revive it ?" "You are mistaken," said Dora. "I never loved you. I was flattered a little at first by your atten- tions, but I did not love you. What might have been, had you acted differently after my reverse of fortune, I cannot say." Looking into her clear, honest eyes, Warner knew that she told. the truth. She had never loved him.</p>
        <p>"You could not expect that I would sacrifice myself for the daughter of a low forger and drunkard, could you ?" he, demaaded, chagrined and annoyed. . "Am 1 any better now?" asked Dora, looking at him keenly. "Yet, just naw, you offered me marriage." Warner bit his lips angrily. "We will not argue," he said. I have made you certain offers. Will you accept them?" "No."</p>
        <p>"YOU prefer suffering and oppression, then? You prefer to be forced into the marriage ?"</p>
        <p>Doia did not answer</p>
        <p>"You love this Sussex chaw-bacon, young Weir. But, by Heaven, he shall never possess you I Mrs. Narr," be added; "take your daugter upstairs and lock her up. We will try your modes of subduing her. She is to have the back chamber, is she not? I'll _warrant that young Weir won't rescue her</p>
        <p>. . Mrs. Narr arose and approached the girl. Dora arose, retreating to the wall, her eyes flashing, her face glowing with a resolute defi- ance.</p>
        <p>"Do not touch me 1" she said, "1 will not go with you 1" "We'll see, my lady," said Mrs. Nam " We'rt, three to one when it conies to that. Jack, lend us a hand."</p>
        <p>• "You'd better give up, Miss Dora,", said Jack, obeying his wife. "I ha'n't got nothing agin you. Why can't you marry Mr. Warner and be happy ?" "Hold your tongue,- Jack," cried Mrs. Narr. "If she wants to hold out, let her. She'll have to yield befor I'm through with her. Seize her other arm, while I take this one. Now. Dora braced her slender figure against the wall, but her pretended parents scizz:d her• so roughly that she was torn from her support: She'struggled with them •wildly for an instant; and then gave utterance to a wild scream.as, they carried her toward the door.</p>
        <p>The scream had not seased to echo through the house when the hall door, which Nahr had omitted to lock, was flung open, and two men rushed into the hall, pa.using on the parlour threshold. The intruders were Lord Champne'y and Sir Graham Gallagher. The scene that met their gaze was sufficiently striking. In the centre of the room two boisterous, half. Intoxicated persons, having in their rude grasp a delicate, struggling girl, whose white face gleamed ' like marble from beneath her crown of dusky hair, and whose wild eyes shone like stars. And at a . little distance, looking on, with a satisfied and exultant smile on his saturnine visage, Felix War- , ner„like a head demon presiding over the girl's i destruction. . Lord Champney saw his kinsrnansfirst. , 1 " Felix !" he ejaculated, in amazement. "You here !" "Champney 1" cried Warner, actually reeling in his utter astonishment. "Champney! Impossibler His face grew livid in its sudden pallor. He' ' gasped for breath. The Narre, appalled at the strange intrusion, and the terror of the leader, released their hold on Dora. The girl gave one wild look up into the handsome face of Lord Champney, now white and stern and strangely rigid, and then she sprang toward him, clasping her hands and crying: "Save me. 0, save me I" She would have fallen at Lord Champney's feet, but that he caught her in his arms. "Save you from what ?" he asked, sternly "From them 1" cried Dora, pointing to the Narrs. , "From him 1" and she indicated Warner. "From him?' repeated the baron. "What has he done to you ?" . "He is going to shut me up—ts compel me to marry him. He is my bitterest testily. He sets those Narrs on against'me—" "He is her persecutor of whom I told you, my lord," said Sir Graham Gallagher, comprehending the matter at once. A flood of light poured in upon Lord Champney's amazed and halt incredulous soul. His counte- nance darkened and grew sterner still as he gazed upon Warner, who, unable to command himself, cowered before him in all the agony of detected guilt. "I see," said his lordship, slowly, a look in his gloomy eyes before which Warner quailed anew. "This is the young lady you knew in Sussex, Felix? This is the young lady you wanted to ' marry? Al's wer. I command sou." Warner was a coward at heart, as one might esh peat an oppressor of women to be. He dared not lie or retuse to answer while those keen, cum- ' manding eyes were upon him. He gasped out an affirmative response. "You Limited her with an infamous proposal, when you discovered her with , these people in London? And when you discovered from them, or otherwise, her actual identity, you determined to force her to marry you? Is this not so? Speak 1" The cowardly villain's looks were sufficient an- swer, without his gesture of assent: "Viper 1" said Lord Champuey, with a stern and awful scorn. "You have stung the hand that cherished you 1 I thought you good and true. But, like all the world, you are base and false, seeking your own ends!" He turned from the humbled villain to the girl clinging to him. He put her gently from him at arm's length, and looked into her face. And as he looked, the sternness melted from his noble visage. The gloom in his eyes lightened. A strange and infinite tenderness quivered about his firm mouth, and beamed from every feature. That pure and innocent face, with its exquisite beauty, its eyes like dusk stars, its tender mouth quivering in a wild and pathetic appeal, all touch- ed his inmost soul. Just such a face as this, pure, sweet and tender, he had loved to think his child would have wern had she lived. It looked to him at that moment like the face of an angel. The father's instinct within him asserted itself, and cried out for the girl's recognition. Not yet, however, did he yield to it. He gently pushed up her sleeve. There was the mark on her arm which he so well remembered— the tiny scarlet cross. At sight of that birth mark, his it doubt—if he still had any—vanished. He caught the wondering girl to his bosom, rain- ing kisses and tears on her pale, astonished face, 1 and calling her by a thousand tender names in his transports of delight. , 'My child! My child !" he whispered, as she . lay trembling against his breast. "You are my own daughter—my little Barbara, whom I thought dead! Heaven has had pity on me at lest! Some brightness has some to my desolate life?" Dora's heart throbbed wildly. "Am I not, then, the daughter of the Narrs ? " she cried. "Am 1 reails your daughter ?"</p>
        <p>ro</p>
        <p>W .0</p>
        <p>"Yes, darling, yes—you are really mine I You were given to:11rs. Narr to nurse by my gocd iend aad physician, Sir Graham Gallagher. And sho has kept y ou.fro,ii me all these years " " And you are Lord Champney, Mr. Warner's kinsman?" questioned the girl, incredulously. "Yes," said Sir Grahain Gallagher, beaming with sympathy. "He is Lord Champney. I recog- nised you last night, by your birth-mark, 3.6 the supposed ciezi.d hress.- I went to Lord Charnpney to-day and told him of my (iiscovery, and brought him home with ins. Your fate has changed since last night my little- buly Barbara I You are no longer homeless and friendless,' but the bearer of an ancient and honourable. name and the heiress of • great wealth !" • "heat of all is the honourable name and the love of parents," sal I Dora, softly, her eyes brimming over with tears. . .</p>
        <p>Lord Chatopney caressed her gently. His heart was too full for words. _</p>
        <p>. By this time the isiarrs were thoroughly and abjectly terrified. The cowardice and fear of Warner had had its due effect, and Sir Graham's remark had plunged :them into deeper depths of terror. .</p>
        <p>." It's all up with us, old woman !" said Jack Narr, in a howling voice. "I always told you how it would end. Here 'tis, tranvortation for life, for keeping the baby that &amp;dint belong to us. Oh, my lord, let 'us tif, and we'll own •up to the truth I We will—we will I It was the old woman did it !"</p>
        <p>"Jack told me to," cried Mrs. Narr, turning on her,spouse furiously. "Our baby died just as we wanted to leave our farm, and Jack and I said we'd let our child be buried as my lord's, and we'd keep my lord's baby for speculation. We thought then of getting a hundred pounds or so, after a time, for giving it up. We did .mean to give her up, my lord. We would have done it before this, only Warner there wanted to marry her, and he offered us five hundred pounds a year if we'd bring about the marriage. I'll make my 'davy that Dors.'s your child, my lord, but don't punish us. If you do," she added, threateningly, "we'll blow on your kinsman, ivir: Warner, and that, won'i, be very pleasant to you." - In the midst of these confessions and recrizni- nations, the hall door again opened, and two men came gliding stealthily is. One of these was the slouchily dressed man Sir Graham Gallagher had remarked at the nearest corner in the afternoon, and pronounced a detective. These men glided psst Lord Champney and Sir Graham into the little parlour, and the detective went up to Jack Narr and laid his hand on his shoulder heavily, exclaiming: "John Narr, you are my prisoner, on a charge of forgery: You must come with us." • Jack utteicel h howl of despair, but that did not prevent the officer from fastening upon his wrists a pair of steel bracelets. " Let us go," said Lord Champney. "This is no place for my daughter. My carriage is waiting in the lane, and the key of the garden door is on the ta;ile."</p>
        <p>Sir Grab dri took up the hey, and Lord Champ-. ney led the way from the house, with Dora cling- ing to his um. ,Warner stole after them, his -face • black with ma!ii;uent pes-,ions. As they paused at the gate to fit the key to the leek, Wart,-: came .up to them, aild said, hissingly: " it's id_ ) with me,-Champocy, but you must allow I game wed. If,this infernal doetoe ha), edit have..earried the girl, and her to you as ray wife and your daughter. ncit there are two bitter drops-in your cup of joy... 'Oce is, that as. you have no on, I shall be heir to your title. The other is, that if yosi have found a lovely daughter, you have a faithless wife. wish you joy ~;f her.' • The door tais 4ia," Warner 'rased out ahead, ti:.te-iing, as he went, the mocking laugh of a toitured yet exultant demon.. The Charnvney carriage was in waiting down thelane. I;he baron handed. 'Dora into it, and then paused ot the step, giving his hand to the court physician.. "We Dart lice to:night, .Sir Graltsm," he said. "I tenet Make Ail haste home. Come and -see me tomorrow. God bless you for all your friendly kindness to me. I shall never forget it." They clasped hands, and the doctor then turned and walked toward his home. .</p>
        <p>Lord Champlicy entered the barouche, and gave the coachman the order: "Home." Then he drew his newly:found daughter close beside him, her head against his shoulder, and tears of mingled joy and despair fell thickly among her dusky tresses.</p>
        <p>His cup ofkjoy seemed full, and yet, as Warner lied said, there was in it a dash of bitterness _that almost Spoiled the draught He had found a daughter—more lovely than he would have dared to picture her—but what was a daughter's love compared to that of the wife he so madly worshipped 1 (2o be continued.)</p>
      </div>
