      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>SOME ARMENIA2i</head>
        <p>SOME ARMENIA2i</p>
        <p>A correspondent, a member of the American National I Red Cro-s ExpGdition, who has recently been travelling through Anatolia. sends us saysthe " Daily C ronicle " some horrifying examples of the rapine and eloodbiled that were the daily commonplaces of his experience. It is impossible, he declares, that the accounts of the mas- sacres can- be exaggerated. He states, moreover, that even the relief party was openly menaced and hooted at by the rabble at Killis where the Moslems were still whetted to the verge of frenzy from the massacre of only ten dap. before,and its safety was only due to the guard of soldiers that accompanied it. At Ourfa, one of the dark, st spcts in all the country, the Turkish soldiery, ailed by the Kurds and a few Circassians, collected the inmates into one apartment,and then one after the other the unhappy Christians were led out and given a blow on the head, like so many cattle at the slaughter. The e.orrepondent adds :—The bodies of the women were robbed of their ornaments, and often stripped of clothing as well. The day following the bodies of the massacred were dragged by the heels out of tbs city by donkeys, and in instances, in the refinement of cruelty, by the hands of the surviving Armenianlads,and thrown altogether into a common trench, or in caves in the mountain side beyond the city walls; but so lightly were they covered with earth that any day one might see the dogs, horribly tat, carrying away an arm, or a font per- haps, to eat at leasnre. Here and there members were to be seen sticking above the ground ; one arm showing distinctly the tate° n3arks indicative of a pious pilgrim- age tn Jerussiem. . . . . Of the ten priests of the kcal Gregorian church only two,the moat illiterate,escap- ed the massacre, and one of these, robbed of his priestly garments and biretta, and clad as a Turk, opened for us , the blackened and charred doors. . . At the end</p>
        <p>_ opposite from the chancel was a large galleryextending the full width of the church, and on the day of the mas- sacre all who could do so had crowded there for refuge. Into this helpless mass of humanity the soldiers and popillacelad fired again and again, and in the body of the church; fearing to injure each other by the use of firearms, they simply struck right and left among the fugitives with axes, clubs, swords, anythin. that castle to hand, killing or wounding all they could": and then, piling up the rolls of matting from the floor and saturat- ing it with petroleum, fire was put to it, and dead and dying were burned together. For hr,urs after the air was filled with the odour of burning flesh and cloth- ing.</p>
      </div>
