        <p>A DREAM'S FULFILMENT.</p>
        <p>By H. SIDNEY WHITE.</p>
        <p>• PART I. . FATE IN FANCY. It was the winter of 1870. France was bravely -11truggling against her German foe ; and while she • -crouched beneath .the irreaistible blows of her •human enemy, even nature seemed to help to make her position more painful. A frost of : exceptional- severity petrified her fields, and Cloaked them with a vast sheet of snow which rendered the awful stains and foot-prihts of battle d.oubly plain. . But in and around the harmless little town of Pont- Sarre the snow, still unsullied, showed only the usual marks of traffic ; and on Christmas Eve of that terrible year, • was . glistening brilliantly beneath the clear blue sky of a most perfect winter morning. Nevertheless, the brightnesSformed such contrast to the misery of the inhabitants of Pont- Sarre, that it -rather. mocked than cheered them. They were mostly women and old men; their sons, brothers, and lovers being under arms, or, alas ! having perhaps already fallen in the conflict. There were brave spirits amoug them, for all that, and the bravest and noblest of them all had suffered as much as any of them, for. she had lost her husband On the battlefield of Sedan. This was the good Madame Gloanec, hostess of the chief hotel of e place, .the 11(itel dis Voyageurs. • No ,at,rdvellers were to be expected at such times, and .she Was only too frequently to -be seen leaning eareleesly against her ever hospitable porch, buried in sombre reverie. This bright winter morning found her thus. Her solid healthy figure had an appearalice of languor, and she looked like a lover disappointed at the trystingldace, and beginning to feel 'Weary of waiting. She was on the point of going indoors, when another female figure appeared in the deserted street and hastened with rapid . Steps towards the Intel. "Au, Julie, at last!" cried Madame Gloanec, "Why so late ?" Julie threw her arms around her friend, and Without a word burst into tears. "But, my dear, my dear," said Madame Gloanec, rocking Julie's head on her capacious bosom. "Why • these tears? Everything goes well. See the beautiful bright day that God has given us. And, my dear, to be worthy of my soldier-son you must be brave. What would Francois say to find you in - tears when he comes back from the war ?" " It is that! it is that horrible ?" muttered a; Lin -beffveen -ifer7iohs. - . • • . Foa. a Moment •neither 'spoke. -Juliem continued her Sobbing, Madame Gloanec her rocking, which ••43he only paused in for a moment while she turned her face to let a tear. fall Without striking Julie. Then Julie suddenly raised her head, and checking ..her sobs said, "What if he never should come back! What if the fate of so many hundreds of thousands should at last overtake him? oh, it is terrible to speak of, but I dreamt it all so vividly - last night, that I Let us enter the house, I cannot tell you here." They went into the warm cheerful kitchen, and sat down side by side, near the stove. - "It was more than a dream," began Julie, with- out waiting to be asked to continue, "it was a - vision. You remember yesterday, in this very morn we were all talking of the detachment of Germans who were expected here with a number of • our brave men under escort, as prisoners ?" • " Yes, certainly; I remember perfectly your disgust because we shall have to house and feed our enemies." "Well, I suppose this worked upon my-feelings, and last night I had a vision.. I came to tell you all about it, but—" "My child, if you are going to relate it, do so withoutstraying from the paint, I pray. Remember, that if he is your lover, he is also my son.' "I will," said Julie, wiping away her last teAr. "As I said, I suppose the news of the approaching Germans, with their priaoners, worked upon my mind so that my thoughts were not at rest upon that subject even in my sleep. All -I. know is, . • that last night, when I fell asleep it appeared to me that I suddenly found myseff outside the old town-gate with all the inhabitants, awaiting the arrival of the cursed Prussians. I seemed °to be quite aware that the Prussians were coming immediately. No one spoke, and I remembered feeling the oppressiveness of the dreadful silence. strained my ears to catch any relieving sound whatever, when I suddenly heard a faint rolling, as of very distant drums. I -looked around me, and . • perceiare3a that everyone had obaerveda.She same sound, by their increased, attentiveigss. The . hound augmented rapidly, until from the faintest humming like the distant ;sound ef a spinning- • - wheel, it increased in a few moments to a roar like the cascades on the river. I felt ne fear, only a strange • -sort of mruinçhdity. Sudd,enly, the - drummers, appeared in sight, ..a'• long, long-line 'of-Prussian soldiers, and French .prisoners behind. Then the toll of the dirttm'e was as loud as the „. loudest thunder. Still, not ,one of us women moved. We blocked up the Whole \read,waai. a I know not a ;a. , but it- seemed teV6 tiaat.it Was my duty to motion. 'When the drummers came so near to us that their drums actually touched some of us, I remember the 'shock- I felt at the sudden way in which they ceased their drumming. Then a fierce officer on horse-back, with his sword drawn, galloped up to where the drummers were stopped by us women, and shouted out, in per- fectly good French, You she-devils! every one of You clear away, or I'll have you shot where you stand.' And then, 0 good Madame Gloanec, you, for the first time, appeared. You seemed in some way much higher- than the rest of us. Waving Your hands in defiance to the Prussian officer, you Shouted, Accursed Prussians! we are all true Frenchwomen, and will not budge an inch to such - dogs as you 1'" "I said that ?" burst in Madame Gloanec. "Well, your dream was indeed. life-like, Julie. And what did the Prussian do then ?" "He frowned like Judas Iscariot, and ordered the nearest soldier to shoot you on the spot." But Julie," cried Madame Gloanec, in horror; " Chut ! Do • not interrupt. I did not say you were shot. Listen ; the soldier took aim at you With his needle-gun, and every second.[ expected to see you fall. My heart beat as if I had been running, as I used to in the old days, when what. was my surprise to see the needle-gun knocked . right out of the soldier's hands." "Knocked out! by whom ?" "Have patience. Imagine, than, my surprise When I saw that one of the prisoners had done it, and that he was no other than Franeoie !" " Ah ! truly your dream is true to life." . "Well, I expected to see him killed at once. But no, the fierce looking officer merely smiled in hia Judas-like way, and pretending to be quite softened, he saluted you, and asked you politely to tell us to move. You refused. Then,' said he, 'food Madame, move out of the way with your friends, and I promise to give _up every French Prisoner I have ; only I mue'st•first pass that gate.' Swear it,' you cried, on the hilt of your sword I' He swore solemnly that what he had said he would do. Then, you turned to us, and waved your. hand, -In an instant we all seemed to be trans- Ported into this street. You and I were standing - tit your' on porch, waiting, as I felt, for the arrival of the prisoners. But the strange-part of it Was that, although it had been daylight a minute -before, now it had become quite dark, with only the light of your flickering lampate enable us to see. In a Moment, we heard the drums age ;u in the same Way as before, only this time the soldiers were Singing v ery loudly their detestable song, 'Germany, Germany,. above all!' HoWeVera you and I felt supremely .happy, for we believed what the officer • . had said." "That I should never have done, take my word 'tor it." • . "The horrible part now comes. 0, Madame Gloanec, if you—" "Well, well, my child, proceed," said Madame Cloanee, impatiently. , " You and I then stood there, both of us quite nappy. • We strained our eyes to catch the • first glimpse of Francois. The drummers had all Marche4,Lpast with their deafening roll, when akithe first prisoners came under the lamps, imagine our. horror as we perceived that all of them were noi, • longer men but—ghosts !" " Ghosts ! • oh! the idea is too horrible," exclaimed Madame Gloanec shuddering. "You tremble already, and there is something Much More horrible to come." "Never mind, my dear,. go on; if was only a cream after all."</p>
