        <p>de Can you hear the rest ?” • "Why not, then?" "It is very horrible ! But it will relieve me to share the horror with you. Weil, suddenly, the savage officer rode up to us, and pointing with his sword at Francois' ghost, he shouted' Come into the market-place we are going to free them there 1' And then—Oh ! the terror of that most horrible part of the dream—then, with a fiendish stnito he threw something on to the.steps at our feet. We both looked down, and saw—O, can you guess? I cannot say it 1° _</p>
        <p>"My dear Julie calm yourself what was it f" "Francois' bleeding head !" "Oh ! Julie stop, no more !" cried Madame Gloance trembling with suppressed emotion. She was a strorg woman, but, like Julie, she was exceedingly superstitious. The two, for a minute, clasped each other without speaking. They seemed afraid of the sound of triedr own voices. Suddenly, Julie looked up.</p>
        <p>" Hark 1" sa:id she listening, "What is that ?" "What ?" said Madame Gloanec, with alarm. "That noise ! oh, it is the rolling of the drums, just as I heard them in my dream 1 0, 711021 Dieu, it is coming true 1"</p>
        <p>PART IL FATE IN FACT.</p>
        <p>A low and distant sound of drumming floated into the silent chamber. Madame Gloance and Julia held their breaths, and with wide open eyes and beating hearts, listened eagerly to that portentous sound. Madame Gloanec regained her self-posses- sion in the presence of what she thought was real danger and her practical mind soon returned to her.</p>
        <p>"My dear," said she, "it may be the Prussians, or it may not be they, but whichever it is there is no reason why a mere dream should make us as timid as children. If your dream came actually true, not a Prussian should see a tear in my eye.' "Alas 1 a lover's love is deeper even than a mother's," gasped Julie. "Nay, say rather, that love and youth are both by nature morbid, even when apart; but when combined betray the strongest natures to weak- ness."</p>
        <p>Julie gazed doubtfully at Madame. In the re- newed silence, the drumming sound became once more audible, this time louder than before. After listening for a moment, Madame Gloanec rose and said, in a voice that sounded quite cheerful after the solemn tones in which they had both been I.,Zt.herto speaking, "Come, Julie, let us, show ourselves Prscek- women. Whatever it be, come with me and face In another minute, they were at the porch. Even. Madame Gloanec shuddered slightly as she involun- tarily glanced down at the stone steps: Julie was visibly affected. Thus it was some moments before they became aware of the nature of the noise which had alarmed them.</p>
        <p>Not Prussian drums were the cause of it, but French kettles, trays, saucepans, and other domestic utensils of a noisy nature. Nor were savage Prussian warriors the drummers, but harm- less little French boys, tramping through the snow in a rough-and-ready imitation of military order. At their head marched Julie's own little brother, carrying a long stick with his handkerchief tied to the top to represent a flag. They halted when they reached the hotel-porch, and for a moment ceased their clatter. Then Julie's little brother, with a perfectly solemn coun- tenance, shouted, "Stand easy I "and came running up to his sister. After a formal salute, without waiting to be asked, he said,</p>
        <p>"80-n jour, Madame Gloanec. Well, Julie, you needn't trouble yourself any more about the Prus- sians. We are going to the old gate to put up a snow sentinel right in front of it. Don't you fear, they're such cowards, that when they see anything like a French soldier, they'll just turn round and bolt. Adieu. Mesdames. En avant! mar-r-rche 1"</p>
        <p>Once more the clatter of pots and pans, which had Tr) alarmed Julie by recalling to her mind the rolling of the drums in her dream, burst forth, and the little urchins tramped on, waving their sham swords, and crying out, "A bas les Prussums I Les Prussiens sont des eltiens !"</p>
        <p>The same evening, soon after sunset, Midame Gloanec and Julie,were seated in the salle d manger of the hotel, at a humble dinner, when suddenly, Julie threw down her knife and cried; "Listen ! what is that? " "it is a horseman," replied Madame Gloanec, "he is coming this way." "He has dismounted at the door," said Julie, excitedly, "I will go." , "Nay, Julie, let me." They both rushed to the door togPther, just as a smart knock resounded through the house.</p>
        <p>• "Madame Gloanec 1" a male voice shotted from the same direction as the knocker.</p>
        <p>"It is Jean," said Maclaine. Gloanec, "He brings news, no doubt."</p>
        <p>Jean was admitted, for'it was indeed he, with a welcome all the more hearty on account of the re- lief his well-known voice had caused. The expres- sion of his face indicated that he harboured important news in his mind, and was quite conenious of it.</p>
        <p>"What is it, Jean ? " asked Madame Gloanec. "lii a moment, good Madame," returned the old man, with a smileof satisfaction. He felt quite re- compensed for his long, cold ride. "My lips are frozen."</p>
        <p>"Come into the salle ; warm yourself, and have some Cognac." "Madame, you are very good," said Jean, enter- ing the room, and seating himself by the stove, while Julie poured him a glass of cognac. "And now, the news! the news ! "</p>
        <p>"Can you not guess ?" asked he, provokingly, eyeing bin cognac. "If I guess, will you tell me whether lam right?" said Julie, who although subdued, appeared to be much affected. "With pleasure Mademoiselle."</p>
        <p>"Then, is it not, that the Prussians are approach- ing, and that you have ridden on in advance to warn us ? "</p>
        <p>"Mademoiselle, it is wonderful ; you repeat the facts word for word." " Alas !" said Julie, with emotion, "I have had intimations from messengers, not earthly. I see further even than this, for, say, am I not right in saying that the Prussians are bringing prisoners with them ? "</p>
        <p>"Mon Dieu 1 "cried Jean, spilling half his cog- nac on its way to his "mouth, with surprise, "Yoa are a.prophetess, mademoiselle.; it is as you say." "And among those prisoners is—is Francois " cried Julie, clutching his arm in her eagerness. "Do not distress yonself without cause,", said Jean, "-I have no news of Monsiem Francois. My whole news ia Simply this, that the prisoners in- clude many from the ranks of his regiment." "It is. all the same !".'eried Julie, her whole figure shaking with excitement., "If my dream has so far come word for word true, why not the rest? Ay, I.have no doubt that he is there. But helnust not enter the town, for the misfortune did not occur until he was there. Nay, he must not enter the town I"</p>
        <p>x:.l,itn.cd Madame Gloanee, see- liberately cloaking herself, where are you going?" 11 :1! ing. to no harm. Do • And before any of rAl_ql(!ed • 'What she was about, the room, and rushed into the</p>
        <p>"But, Julie.' ing that she V ' "what do you</p>
