      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <p>f'</p>
        <p>XA</p>
        <p>have—has fainted, and is now lying half4rOzen in the snow. Give us the loan of a couple of your soldiers to bear herinto my house." As she finished speaking, one of the French pri- soners, who was near the head of the column, and had been watching these proceedings with anxiety, forced his way to her. She, at sight of him, threw up her arms and shrieked, "Ah, mom Franfoie I" while be, scarcely noticing her (filial as he was) threw himself on the ground beside the swooning daughter of Pont Sarre. " Alas !" he moaned, find my Julie dying." The Major was, like most Germans of his posi- tion, a gentleman in every sense of the word. He grasped at a glance the state of affairs, and naming two reliable men, ordered. them to carry the lady ' into the town, and bring back the French prisoner, whom .he would allow to accompany them.- • The German soldiera advanced to obey the order, quietly pushing aside .the inquisitive old women, in, whom curiosity overcame fear. They found Fran- cois lying across Julie's laxly. One of them, stoop- ing, tried to rouse him, telling him in kind tones that he might accompany them._. But he gave no heed to their kindness. Again the German stooped to rouse him, and this,time discovered the cause of his silence: he too was unconscious. He had been suffering from slight concussion of the brain from a wound in the head, and this great emotional shock had easfly stunned him. . The soldiers turned to inform the. Major of .this, who at once ordered two more men to carry Francois to the same lioue. He knew that every house in the town would have some of his soldiers quartered in it that night, and, therefore, that Francois would be safe,</p>
        <p>"it is she. I return to</p>
        <p>Thus, then, were Frauf:ios and his beloved Julie borne into the town side by side, followed by the good 'Madame Gloanee, whose courageous nature even could not altogether bear up against this over- whelming emotion. .</p>
        <p>In due tinie, the column of German soldiers and French prisoners were dismissed to their allotted quarters, and the 'Major with the doctor turned towards the Rad des Voyageurs, which was to be the headquarters. In the open doorway • they found to their surprise, the very woman who had spoken up so bravely at the old town-gate. ' "Welcome to my house," said she, extending :both hands towards them;. "for although you may be bearing arms against my native land, you have bound me personally, by a life-long obligation." The Major,- like many a hardy warrior, was as sensitive as any girl. Ile enquired about the par- ticulars of the case; and, When he heard the -whole story froils Madame Gloanec's eloquent lips, he was gladof the darkness that concealed its effectupon his countenance. After a short pause, he turned to the Doctor, and, speaking in German, asked to him to go and see whether Francois' state of health would justify him in leaving him beb:nd, on the morrow. In a few minutes the doctor" returned with an expression on his face of great mental excitement. Addressing the Major, with unwonted emphasis, he said,</p>
        <p>" My dear Major, quite a lovely romance! Fran- cois' betrothed has recovered, and is now nursing Mm, for he is still unconscious. But, Major, of all the beauties I ever saw in my life, she is the most beautiful. It made me feel quite envious of that lucky dog of a Frenchman, although poor fellow he is very-far from out of danger. But you are to be envied -too, Major, for in this little drama, you have the power of arranging the fate of at least one of the characters."</p>
        <p>"Nay, doctor, that depends upon you, fdr Your report of his condition settles it," _</p>
        <p>" What! It does ? " asked the doctor, quite triumphantly. "Of course. If he is in danger of his life, I can- not move him.</p>
        <p>"Then,, he is! he is, "cried the doctor, "it would be certain death to move him."</p>
        <p>And, as he said it, those Iwo battle-stained, old soldiers, clasped hands, and shook them, with the joy of gratified children.</p>
        <p>Willa happened the next day, when, after care- ful nursing, Francois returned to consciousness, and found himself alone with his Julie, it would be quite supererogatory to describe. Julie's dream was fulfilled almost literally. The difference between the two conclusions' that Of the dream and that of the fact, was caused by the cor- relative divergence, which ever exists, between morbid fancy and logical evolution. , It is just in the scenes and occasions of the blindest rage and most unreasonable hatred caused by war, that so often occur the, noblest, most generous, and most humane of episodes which the iourse of the self-engrossed world can display. Simply, for .the reason that these events, whose interest is so expansive, overwhelm the egotism of a single unit in their performance. What a mass of unrecorded heroism lies irreclaimably buried in the mountainous pecords of what may be fitly called the blood and thunder ot war ! As one rescued from the oblivion which conceals so many, this simple little Idyl of the Wars is offered.</p>
        <p>PEOPLE should mind how they express themselves at public dinners. We read, the other day, that the builder of a church now in course of erection in New York, when the toast of his health was given, rather enigmatically replied that he was "more fitted for the scaffold than for public speaking." Fr is said whca a. Deadwood deacon gets angry over a game of poker and drops his opponent with a pistol he is not, allowed to escape. A meeting of the congregation is called, and in nine cases out of ten he is censured for being so impulsive.</p>
        <p>A KNOWING OLD LADY.—An aged Scotch lady, knowing the habits of her old and spoilt servant, when she wished a note taken without loss of time, held it open and read it over to him, saying, "There noo, Andrew, ye ken a' that's in it; noo llama stop to open it, but jest send it off."</p>
        <p>Two gentlemen were lately examining a portion of a plough in a market-place. " bet five dollars," said one, "you do not know what this is for." "Done," said the other; "it is for sale." The bet was won and the wager paid. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>PASHA'S 'EXPEDIENT.</head>
        <p>THE PASHA'S 'EXPEDIENT.</p>
        <p>When Assad Marouf was Pasha of Grand Cairo, in Egypt, he was often at his wits' 'end in planning for the raising of mouey—a situation not at all un- familiar to the potentates of the Ottoman Empire. But Assad was well read in the story of Antiquity, and he called to mind an item of the old history that he thought he could bend to his purpose. In his domain were many v.n-y wealthy Jews, and he issued an order that .on Oe following -day, at a given hour, the most opulent of them should be brought before him.</p>
        <p>Tire hour arrived, and the Jews were present—a score or more—trezetbling in dread apprehension before the magnate whe held their lives in his hand. They 'approached the 4-e:Ad divan in mortal terror; for they saw an ominous elnud upon his dark brow.</p>
        <p>"What ho I—ye Infidels !". the Pasha exclaimed, like one whose wrath is deep and fervent, "I have been reading the annuals of my fathers, wherein it is plainly set forth, that your fathers did borrow of them, pledging their lives .in surety of payment, divers large sums of money and valuable jewels, and merchandise, and plate; and 0, ye Cafires (rascals)—that loan which our fathers made to your Infidel ancestors has never been repaid? Begone from my sight ! I leave it for you to find the record, and make up the account. Bring to me the full payment within eight-and-forty hours, or, by the beard of the Prophet! we'll see !" _ _ __ _</p>
        <p>The terrified son; of Israel hastened homeward; .and, however they May have found the account, they returned on the morrow with a burden of gold that caused the Pashato forgive them the old debt; and he instructed his secretary to give them a receipt in ample form. . .</p>
        <p>THE BEST MAGAZINE.—In reply, to a young writer who wished to know which magazine would give him the highest position quickest, a contem- porary advised "a powder magazine, if you contri- bute a fiery article." PU4NCTU.kTION.-=-A stranger in a printing-office asked the youngest apprentice what his rule of punctuation was. "I set up as long as I can hole my breath, and then I put a comma; when I gape, I insert a semicolon; and when I want to sneeze, I make a paragraph." . _</p>
        <p>AN eloquent orator proposes to "grasp a ray of Hunt frvn the great orb of day, spin it into threads et gold, and with them to weave a shroud in which to • warp the wnirlwind which died upon the bosom of our western rairics." We fear, the machinery 'will break be-Lore ti m; fabric is thiough the loom.</p>
