      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>LADI E$ LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADI E$ LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR N ELLI L,</p>
        <p>Very big hats have been entirely discarded by stylish women for the prznent, and next to the toque the pointed felt shapes are most in favour; while for girls in their teens, or even of a more mature age, provided they have round, youthful. looking faces, the plain round-brimine,l hats with crowns of medium height are much the I:twt.</p>
        <p>A very plain bat in detail, but withal attractive, is in a cedar brown tone of felt. This is decked with an erect end of Indian-hued ribbon and accordion kilted rosettes of rose-coloured and green shot silk, while the white and green cock's feathers are arranged at one side. • •</p>
        <p>The new evening -b-o-r-lico, cut square in front. high at the btck, nto long-,-leeve4 has succeeded beyond its creators most :titguine anticipations. A Marie Stuart collar, often of flashing diamonded lace, frames a white swan-like throat and enhances the charm of a well.coillurea bead.</p>
        <p>A most becoming bodice can be made of green plaid glace made in the simplest form. Another material is rich moire silk, with large pink flowers and leaves in a blurred chine design on it. A beautiful evening gown is made of rich ivory. satin, and the attraction lies in some very elaborate embroidery which should appear on the front centre panel in the skirt on the front of the bodice, and on the epaulettes. The embroidery is in a graceful conventional design, the scrolls of which are formed with small pearls, and the important parts of the pattern are worked out in clusters of irides- cent paste diamonds, which are exceedingly brilliant, studded here and there with large turquoise beads. The side panels should be faced with turquoise-blue velvet, and a narrow band of the same goes round the waist. A soft frill of ivory-white lace falls on the • arm under the embroidered frills, and a ruche of the same goes round the neck. The epaulettes ought to be lined with turquoise-blue velvet, and there is a cluster of pink rases on the left shoulder.</p>
        <p>A very Beautiful model is designed in ivory satin, and the bodice is embroidered across the front in a very graceful manner. There are no trimming or if trimming are added they should be of creamy lace, or insertions may be aJade both back and front. The sleeves are a decided novelty, forming a kind of frill going over the shoulder.</p>
        <p>Children and very young girls generally wear evening dresses which are almost, if not quite, high at the neck, but it is pretty to have them cut out just enough to allow of a string of pearls or pretty beads being worn at the throat, or a little pendant or other ornament on a fine gold chain; and either of these ornaments make a suitable present. B,el -7 -nit doubt, the most fashionable Colour ior euildrer.'s wear, bur, it is bright red of the Post-office tint, and the deeper shades verging on cardinal are not recognised at all. Poppy-red, it it usually termed, but it is the old red poppy of the corn-field, and not its garden rival' of artistic hue. Black fur, braid, and buttons are all tv:=ed on outdoor wraps to tone down the brilliant tint, and collars of veiv.t edged black fur, are especially useful where red •is not becoming in too close' proximity to the complexion. The prettiest bonnets for tiny girls are in the close Dutch shape, but the brim in.t over the forehead, instead of being severely 'hal, tni phtin, breakm into two boi--pleats, which reveal a bright lining, arid set very lull, by reason of the edg!ng of fur. A band of good ecru inserti..:n is turned (back on the. bonnetS and there is usually a b; oad Alsatian bow at the; top. _ With a grey-blue velvet pelisse, a bonnet made from the outings could be edged with narrow' grey fur, the front lining Of pale rose-pink, and ecru lace turned back on the bonnet.</p>
        <p>Velvet and velveteen blouses and skirts are having quite an extraordinary run this winter. Dressy, blouses of plain or figured ,material, made up on a boned and fitted lining, !Iva-a or loss elaborately trimmed with lace or pisementerie, are used for reception dresses, at tho theatre, and fbr denzi-toilette generally., :Velvet and velveteen shirts are favourite indoe • bodices, and cyclists 'patronise them with fervour. Wonderfully pretty are the fancy velvets and vel4teens employed. One of the most effective velveteens has -a white ground--white with , black spots—or coloured ground with white or black spots. Among all the printed velveteenc tilt:lse in the white ground take the palm foJ elegance, especially when the pattern is an Oriental or Paisley design. . Your loving</p>
