      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>ONE MOMENT PLEASE!</head>
        <p>ONE MOMENT PLEASE!</p>
        <p>tlffere-s from Gravel. Lumbago, Piles, Pains in the t)r.psy Wind and Water Complaint, Diseas.• Kilr6, a Bladder Stone, Sciatica, Rheumatism and Gout will find a po.itive Cure in Holdroyd's Gravel Pills. Try a small box and It not satisfied your money will be returned. Prices Is. Of all Chemists, or post free for 12 stamps from NOLDROYD*9 Medical Hall Checkheaten, Yorks. Sold at 35, Bridge-at, Warringtm. Don't be put off, if you cannot get them *rite othe Proprietorland abcxwill be sent Next cost - kclvt</p>
        <p>24 596.</p>
        <p>" OIVI EN'S CFI AT.'</p>
        <p>, • •., o•a that ear!v .1:: the New Year, her Majesty p- • • - :U., king :•.on-dra restorations at Carl:fir-64e mi mory ot Prince Henry et Battenherg. The in in:sightly ruin is the rocil-.ss Chapel of St. Nicholas, mm although oily built about sixty years ago,stands te. sight of a Saxon church,and its complete restora- is an important feature in the Queen's project. It ,ic;pated that the memorial to the late Prince Henry in W'bippiegham Church, ar, WO, by the way, will be noteworthy for its antique design, and considerably add to the interest and beamy of the building.will be ready for dedication in February next. Until the firs year of her mourning has passed, Princess Henry will not, of course,make any public appearance, hut as G)vernor of the Isle of White it has been intimated that she will at- tend one or two semi-public functions, having a philan- thropic aim, before leaving Osborne for the Riviera. Prince Alexander of Teck, whose heroic crnduct in the field is the theme of general admiration throughout Mat• abeleland just now, is the third and youngest blother of the Duchess of York, and only twenty-two years of aue. In various difficult corneis the voting Prince seems to have behaved hints'elf with great coolness anu dash,par- ticularly in the fight at Walsjas Kraal, when he led his troop of the 7th Hussars through a most dangerous posi- tion, and practically by his energy and military acumen, won the whole fight. The Duchess's eldest brother, Prince Adolphus, of Teck, is aide-de-camp-to the Com- mander-in-Chief, and her other brother. Prince Francis, his recently been appoiqted aide-de-camp to Major- General Galbraith,C.B., commanding the Quetta District of India.</p>
        <p>Of 1..t0 years the old fashioned style in Christmas liter- ature has been gradually disappearing, but this season the majority of the periodicals which claim to he special Christmas productions would do equally well for spring, summer or autumn. There is, it is true, some attempt to ma:Le up for the deficiency, in pidtorial effect, but in many instances this is overdone,and spoilt by too highly coloured efforts. Most of the illustrated magazines have frankly abandoned the idea of celebrating Yule-tide,and apparently with the object of crowding in stories of loathsome and blood-curdling adventures. Opinions greAtly differ as to favourite Christmas stories, but it is diffirult to believe that the great in Ajority favour the change which unmistikablv has come over the periodi- cals during the last ten years.</p>
        <p>Apropos ef Yule-tide, it does not appear to be gener- ally known that the Yule-log is a relic ef Druidism. Its name is believed to be a (torruption of the wheel-log, a wheel in Druidical Symbolism typifying the march of the sun. The lighting of the Yule Are is reminiAcent of the sacred fires kindled by the Druids at mid winter in the round towers, which yet remain in many parts of Great Britain, Ireland, France and Spain. The new wadded and quilted Jaoanese dressing gowns are particularly desirable garments, and if there still remain any of my reader: who have New Year's gifts weighing on their minds they cannot do better than sally forth in search of one. They reach the zenith of comfort, but of course are not so smart as could be wished, owing to the wadding. However, as they are intended for the bedroom where comfort is the primary consideration, the latter is not much of a drawback.</p>
        <p>Fins fur little people are especially dainty this season, and quite inexpensive. Some are built of gauze and spangled with shot sequins to match, while others are composed of quills in different colours, painted with tinsel paint in a pretty scroll design round a small medallion in the centre. A smart little satin case, em- broidered to match, and suspended by silken cords, Is sold with each fan to hold it when not in use.</p>
        <p>Flounces or not flounces, that is the question, and one of the burning questions of fashion at the present mo- ment. For day-wear our skirts are, so far, plain as of yore. but for evening, wear there is little doubt that this particular form of trimming is creeping into favour. I have rocently seen a number of silk and satin slips, covered from heel to hip with graduated and kilted flounces, manufactured of a substantial silk gauze, so hemmed that the edges stood out like the crinkled petal of a flower, but I much prefer the more artistic silk or satin skirts which beast one full, deep-knee flounce ruched to produce a frou-frou at the foot. They are much more becoming in every way. In many instances the personal experiences of the re- cent earthquake in England were very extraordinary, one of the most r3diculous of these recorded being that of a schoolmaster.who, hearing the things rattling about in the boys' room overhead, went up and caned the lot, for romping and disturbiag his rest. Another individual was roused by an unusual noise made bv his parrot, which when he went downstairs was lnudly calling " Thief, thief !" There ia no doubt that if the disturb- ance bad occurred during the daytime instances ot its early perception by animal life would have been ob- served, for it is well known that many animals show signs ,of restlessness before an earthquake. In different parts of South America domesticated animals are kept for the purpose, and can, it is said, be confidently re- lied upon te give timely warning, of the approaching disturbance. According to Professor Milne who is one of the great authorities on the subject, pheasants are among the mo:t sensitively affected,and feel the tremors before they are noticeable to human beings. As yet motdr cars are objects of curiosity, serving little purpose except as something to talk about. Only very few have seen a mechanically propelled vehicle,but there has, however, been time enough for accidents. The other day one of these contrivances when speeding along a leading thoroughfare in London, suddenly caught fire, and the day before a simi4 accident occlrred in Paris, the flames from the oil tz, bursting out to a great height. The unfortunate`mdividual in charge of the London motor cor was of course greatly chaffed by the passing omnibus and cab drivers, and it was " the jokes finally put out the fire." But what many people are seriously asking is, what is going to happen when motor cars come into general use, and the r )ads are crowded with them P So long as they only burn it does not mat- ter much to passers-by, especially in the cold weather, it is their liability to blow up that is the more terrify- ing, and unless there is to be some official check to care- lessness and recklessness, a cnns;derable number of us stand an excellent chance of fervently wishing the motor car had waited another century or so before careering wildly about the streets.</p>
        <p>APPLE JELLY —Quarter core, and stew one pound of apples, in a syrup tnade of a teacupful of water and one pound of lump ugar, which has boiled five minutes. Take nu l• the apples and place them neatly into a but- tered meuld Soak pieviously an Ounce of gelatine and add to the syrup let it boil a little, and when slightly cooled pour into the mould. Turn out when cold and serve with custard.</p>
        <p>" MADGE."</p>
        <p>REPAIRING THE MACHINE.</p>
        <p>We never wonder. when we see a machine gradually</p>
        <p>wearing out. We expect it. If it bhould run sear after year,and show no signs of wear—well,we should wonder at that. Constant friction destroys the substance wf a wheel or an axle, and it is never replaced. When the time comes there must be a new whee) or a new axle:</p>
        <p>The human body is a machine, built to run without stopping for an instant. For tf it, stops once, there is no ' way to start it again. Hence it must be repaired while in motion. And is it? The matorialifor , this purpose we take into our stomach as food. Sometimes we are silly enough to imagine that nature makes it necessary to eat that we may have the pleasure of eating. Put the case the other way, and you will have it right. It is necessary to eat in order to repair the body,to replace waste material; and she makes the act pleasant so we may not neglect it. That's the idea.</p>
        <p>Thus we go on. Nerves, muscles, bones, and flesh wear out gradually, and are replaced as fast as they wear out. So we get to regarding our bodies as indes- tructible—the same thing, you know, right along. About once in seven years the body is completely changed. It isn't the sarne one you had. But you never noticed when one went out and the ot her came.</p>
        <p>But when a person talks likes this,what is the matter? "I got dreadfully thin and was gradually wasting away." A woman says it—Mrs. Mary Elizabeth Roberts of the Temperance Ho'el, 51, Market-street. Holyhead, in a letter dated June 22nd, 1893. This wasting goes with different diseases, and always with consunzpiion. It is fatal then, to be sure: We all know that. The doc- tors have not yet found any way to cure it. We should be glad indeed if they could cure it. For oh ; how many perish from it ; they fall like grass before a scythe. Well, Mrs. Roberts goes on to say :—" All who saw me thought I was in a decline. I was very low-spirited and tormented with groundless fears, and I became so nervous that I could not bear the slightest noise. Later on, a dry, hacking cough set in, accompanied by night sweats. In this condition I continued year after year I took various medicines, including cod liver oil, but they did me no good. I saw doctor after doctor, but they gave me no hope of recovery. "When I was 24 years of age two 2doctors told me I was in a consumption, ani nothing could be done for me. This m.de me give up all hope, and I got weary and tired of life. But I dragged along; at one time able to be about, and again having to lie helpless On the couch."</p>
        <p>Thus far we perceive that Mrs. Roberta seemed to have all the symptoms of consumption, and probably no doctor would have taken any other.view of her case. In fact, judging by the signs, what other view was it possible to take? Still, before we approve that ver- diet,let us read the beginning of her letter, and then the en iof it. We have as yet read only the middle of it.</p>
        <p>"From childhood," she says. "I was never strong— always tired and weakly. I had a poor appetite, bad taste in the mouth, pain at the right side, and frequen palpitation of the heart. Often I could not stand for giddiness, and I fainted with the least excitement."</p>
        <p>This was up to the time the apparent signs of con- sumption were apparent. What followed that, we know. Now for the end :—"ln April, 1891, I got a' bottle of Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup, and beon to use it. This I did because I read of CAietil like mine having been cured by it. I bought.the medicine from Mr. Wilson. Drug Half,Holyhead. In a few days I felt a little relief. My appetite was better and my food agreed with me. I kept on taking it, and slowly, but surely, regained my strength, and in three months I felt better than ever in my life befom Since then I have been in good health, and able to do any kind of work. I desire others to know what has given me back my lif.? an./ health. You may print tais statement and I will answer all en- quiries."</p>
        <p>Now what have you to say? Her disease was not consumption, was it? No, not at all. But it .was indigestion and dyspepsia ; from inherited tendencrt no doubt ; a disease which so closely resembles consump- tion that even skilled physicians are constantly dPceived and treat it wrongly—alas! with, too often fatal results. Assisted by Mother Seigel's Syrup, which nor- recteC th. torpid stomach and liver, and expelled the poison from the blood, nature rebuilt her wasted machinev, put back the wartu flesh upon the bones, filled the muscles with new vigour, and said to the aforesaid sufferer,. " Now live, work and be happy !"</p>
        <p>What a disease to watch out against ! What a remedy to cure it when ignorance or neglect has allowed it to secure a lodgment!</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>W ARHIN GTo N CuNG IVATIVI W 0 BKING-11 EN'S • ANNUAL BALL.</p>
        <p>The a'love 'nail was held on Aonday evening in the Assembly Room at the Conservative Club. The attendance was much smaller than usual. No al- teration waq made in the appearance of the ball- reom, the decorations for last Thursday's assembly having been untouched, but on this occasion the music for dancing was supplied by Mr. Robert An- derton's band, which played remarkably well for a programme containing 22 dances. Dancing com- menced shortly after eight o'clock, and was thoroughly enjoyed for several hours. Alderman Francomb, J.P.. C.C., Alderman Wright, J.P., C mn- cillor Smethurst, and Mr. Ephraim Aukland offi- ciatel as M.C.'s, and Mr. Robert 11. Daintith. the hon. sectretary to the Ball Committee, rendered valuable assistance in many ways. The supper, which was again supplied by Mr. and Mrs. Watkins was an excellent one, and was thoroughly enjoyed.'</p>
