      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>AN ADOPTED GIRL'S HARD LOT.</head>
        <p>AN ADOPTED GIRL'S HARD LOT.</p>
        <p>A BED IN A CUPROkRD AND MEALS IN THE COALHOUSE.</p>
        <p>A gentlem• n farmer named Charles Brown, of CAM.. ' bois was fined £lO and £8 ss. costs at Blyth on Mori— day for cruelty towards iliaSimnson,aged twelve and a half years, his aderted child. The girl said she was compelled tio sleep in a dark cupboard under the stairs on a mattress, with only saeking to cover her, while she had to take her meals, consisting of cold potatoes with bones to pick,in the coalhouse. Defendsnt had kicked her on the thigh. She was compelled to rise at half-past tour ill ttn- • Arorning,and did not go to bed before eleven i o'clock at n ;lat. She had to milk several cows, and she never got out of the farmstead. She never attended school or church, and she Cad to wear farmer's boots, and stockings with no feet to '!sm. The defendant de- nied the whole of the allegations and declared that th child was very wilful. She cut her bot,ts with a knife, and misbehaved herself in an a tonishing dei4rfte Thor had treatsd her well, thnugh he admitted be had com- mitted a fauit in not sending her to school.</p>
        <p>Headley Park. Ensom, the property of Mr. Mappin. the firm of Niappin and Webb, has been dsAtroved by fire. Miss Sarviant, the housekeeper, is missiniz, and iv blieved to have perished.</p>
        <p>The Birkenhead Watch C(mmittee on Monday investi- gated severfl charges against police officers, and it was resolved to reduce an inspector with twenty years' son. vice to the lowest rank of constable. Another inspector was censured. At Manchester, in counectinn wi'h charge against. a man named Tarinesewhn had been arrested at Birkenhead after ahscnnding.Superintendent Bannis er as accused of withholding the warrant for his arrest. In remanding Taylor the magistrates said a mullen of maney,nuld nnt serve for ball. On the eigtitet anniversary of the deatk.pf the Grand Dbiche9stes4e,the Empress Frederick,iiirbsu•a went over to D,radt. 11.jesty was received at the station hi- 'he Grand Duk-.with whom she drnr. tn the maus.leutn on tlis linsenhohe, and laid a beautiful ' wreath at thn foot of her sister's tomb. The Emp-ess never fails to rie- the tribute of affection in mem wv ef her favourate and beloved sistcr. Her Majesty is sti'l R. vigorous horse-woman, and rides daily, at least, twieP, tor a couple of hours at a stretch. She visits all the folks nu her estate, ard atm: S great interest in her pet institutions at Cronherg. At Friedrichshof, there are no " State" apartments, all the rooms in the Castle 'ere tnet.nt for use,although a Royal enrteze could be accpm- rn eated if neces,ar, . The main aPProiloh is off a ! rood with -fin'', wrought-irs'n cntrance gates, that hospi. tab', exhibit to the bona fide traveller a cheerfol glitnrum 'of the grounds.</p>
