      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>TONEY FELrx's CIRCUS.</head>
        <p>TONEY FELrx's CIRCUS.</p>
        <p>A more than usually entertaining programme has been submitte,4 at the above popular place of amusement each night this week, arid consequently the audiences have been very large. Clever riders, farcical clowns, gymnasts, &amp;c., occupy the first part of the evening,and then comes the great water carnival, the ficst of its kind ever seen in Warring- ton. The audience have fir-t presented to them a village smithy, where the blacksmith's trade is be- ing industriously plied. Then appear two typical pan*.omime harlequins who appropriate everything they can lay hands on. A function in the shape of a village wedding is succeeded by the usual festivi- ties,and the main item takes the form of a picnic to a miniature island. The arena is laid with water- proof canvas, and with this accompli,h.-d the tepid water i. allowed to enter, and in half-a-minute 80.000 gallons are poured forth. The wedding party make their appearance, and boats are launched, but. the two renegades tske possession of the island,and here the amusing part of the entertainment is to be seen. Futile attempts are made to dislodge them from their position, and in return the parties are thrown into the water A general melee ensues,and the splashing and shouting is tremendous fun. Some dozen persons are to be seen strugg:ing in the water together, and the sport winds up in confu- sion. Mr. Felix is to be warmly commended for his enterprise in bringing before the public such a unique and enjoyable performance. Its Very novelty draws a crowd with which the jocular proprietor, Toney Felix, is growing more and more a fey ,urite. Ku..v.--New white !nom nie Cement for Pottery, Poreeki,n. ,',6d. ad is, postfige 2d Of hamlets. METHODIST t'ks7ll4 CHURCH, BUCKLEY STREET.</p>
        <p>A concert was given the above plate on Satur- day evening in aid of th- tuad for J4mes Bate's children. The children and pat,nts of Bate were present. The Chairman, lfr. H. Ti.ornton, in his address, explained how the children had been left destitute, an' i asked all present who had not signed the pledge to taken lessoa from ths late events and sign the pledge at once. The concert was then opened by Miss Woolley, giving the favourite song,": T',e &amp;vier Zee." Miss A Morrell sang "Loch Lomond" with good eff w.t. and was encored. She then ga, e" I Coal C mid I?" Mrs. Moores contributed " Tell it to Jesus" A se- lection on the violin and piano, hy Messrs. Allen an F,ster, gave satisfaction. Mr. Furness anau-ed the audience by his clever recitations in the Lanca- shire dialect.." Abo'th' Yate's Speech." Mr. Taylor's song," Happy moments,".received muc`i applause Mr. tlannan brouiht, the peogratn,ne to a close by rendering" Iha Holy City with great taste. A. vot ea. thanks was passel, on the motion of Mr. Trow, seconle.i by Mr. ewitt, to the performers and the Chairman for their serviees, am; to Mr. John Bramwood, tor the manner in which he had worked to make the concert a suceess. The. Chairman re- turned thankF, ani the concert was brought to a close with the Doxology.</p>
      </div>
