      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>TIIE EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>TIIE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>TEWRSDAY, DECEMBER 24th, 1898.</p>
        <p>•IT is rather singular that the only A boubtful evict's threat of international Prospect. strife which disturbs the end of the yeir should come from the other side of the 4tlantic, just as it did lost year. The United States is a wealthy country and is powerful in the broadest Eense of the word, though its power at the present time dces not take the tangible form huge armies or navies. Hitherto the p3ople of th 6 States have been of Milton's way of thinking, for they have been co*Lent to believe that peace hath her victories no less renowned than war. It</p>
        <p>Ilcryv looks as though a great change is coming over</p>
        <p>the spirit of that nation. The Americans do not kenrto be satisfied with their triumphs in the in ikatrial and political spheres; they are beginning to hanker after military glory. At the end of last Year President CLEVELAND'S references to Great britain in his Message to Congress were neither oiore nor less than a menace to this country; at the end of the present year it is Spain which is teceiving the too ardent attention of the politi- tutus at Washington. The Venezuelan question, is 4011, fortunately, on the way to settlement, but there can be no doubt that when President CLlavzi..e.xn claimed, in an almost offensive way, the right to intervene in a dispute between Atigland and the Spanish-American Republic, he eiltered upon a path which might have led to disastrous results both for his own and for this country. An Anglo-Ametican conflict has however been avoided. After the first gust of excitement ‘as over it was seen that Jingoism was not quite so strong either in the states or in this country as it sometimes seems to 14e. In America the PRESIDENT was severely can- %tired even by members of his own party for his waxlike language, and when the time came for %gain nominating candidates for the Presidency, Ir. CLEVELAND'S name was scarcely ever men- tioned. In this country,although We had in pole r bat is described as a "strong Tory Government ," Ilogoism was singularly mute. Once again it was :leen that there is a vast difference between Tories 111 office and Tories out of office. When out of °face they clamour for "strong measnres " en the %lightest provocation, but when in office they (lo liot figure quite so valiantly. They are prompt kid vigorous in their dealings with such persons ki Ring PREMTICH of Ashantee or the " Fuzzy- Wuzzies " of the Soudan, but there is ,a woaderful</p>
        <p>hiannnt of discretion, not to say timidity, in their Reneral foreign policy. Lord SALISBURY wrote a 4%patch or two in a pretty incisive style, but in the end he backed down a step isr two and was 4ble to patch up an arrangement with Mr. OLNEY Itt Washington. No sooner, however, was the United States out 424 one international complication than it fell into anOther, and it is at least questionable whether this latest one willho got rid of without the burn- Ing of gunpowder. It is evident that there is anziongst a certain section of Americans strong YLOPatliy with the inhabitants of the island of \4lba who are struggling desperately to free them- 4elvos from the control of the Spaniards. In the 'Present instance, however,PresidentCLlMßLAND is ct,the restraining and not the inciting authority. -uhe filends of the Cubans appear to be strongly Npreseuted in Congress, but the PRESIDENT is determined that the States shall net go to war in SPain as long as he is in uffice unless his hands Ile forced in some unexpected way. That any further interference in this struggle ineVitaldy</p>
        <p>11leans a war with Spain is now quite understood. ble Spaniards make no secret of their intehtion t° fight for Cuba to the last gasp, sot) speak, and Itgainst all corners. This, therefore, is really the War cloud, and the only war-cloud so far as can be Itorl, which hangs over the world at the end of *lt is, as we have said, very singular that the ullited States of 911 countries should again figure</p>
        <p>4t the end of another year as the most militant of the nations. It is not by any means a reassuring 4Pectacle. Before many years are over there will .Lse at least a hundred millions of people in the lunited States and this immense population will e at command, in the highest state of develop- all the resources of eivilisat iOn. If the3e re- -Ik3nrces are to be used for. warlike . purposes the ro3pecC is not one that can be viewed with any elings of pleasure.</p>
        <p>Those v,'ho are interested in the °,,enarai 131 )th's ria scheme. doings of the Salvation Army will find a lot of useful informa- tion in some " Notes" on the social work of the iktrny in 1896, which have been arranged in the °fllof a ,pamphlet by Mr. BRAMWELL Boon" In :17i2,0rously written introduction, General BOOTH the work that has been done in connec- with his Darkest England Scheme. He i7lotains that this work, which was commenced it a Corner of the Metropolis, is rapidly spreadi3g i:elf throughout the world, and is already reveal- ftß its valu, to the community in ahundred forms. to ill subsid.s2d by seventeen different govern- an thQrs3 are now in connection with it separate institutions in different parts of the</p>
        <p>world. "It is true," he adds, " that we have been so far thwarted with respect to the Over-Sea- Colony part of our scheme by the selfishness and greed of men, mostly of the class who talk loudest and longest of loving their fellows, but who ois sadly short of the practical side of their theory when the promotion of the interest of their poorer and less favoured brethren appears at all likely to involve some interference with their own.' He has, however, still one bold generous offer, awaiting further inquiry "which is not likely to be defeated by selfish and ignorant prejudice." This appears to be his last chance of establishing his over-sea colony under the British flag, and if it turns out to be a disappointment, he will have to re-consider some of the offers that have been made to him elsewhere. He still finds great interest manifested in this scheme in various countries,but at the same time he deplores that many of the early friends of the enterprise have left him to struggle forward without their countenance, sym- pathy, and help. He gives various reasons for this falling away. The first is that human nature is fickle. "Some people are," he says, "so con-: atituted that they cannot hold to any one thing for long together. Eipecially is this the case with regard to their philanthropy. As the children are ever wanting a new amusement, so their elders soon tire of old fields of usefulness, and turn their faces and bestow their favours on something new.' Then, again, "the fact that we have not invited every friend who gave a £5 note or made a speech in favour of the plan at the onset, to join in the management of it, has influ- enced others." Some have deserted on account ot religion—either because the thing was not managed in favour of. some particular religious denomination, or, on the other hand, because it was not governed on atheistic or, at least, " any- thingarian " principles. In spite of all drawbacks, however, General Booms assures his friends that the scheme is net only still alive but vigorous and going ahead quietly and surely "td become all that was ever promised for it and probably a good deal more."</p>
        <p>Those who enjoy the exhilarating exercise of skating have been able during the past week to engage in their favourLe pastime. But there are '1 some who are unable to wait until the ice is suit- able, and a number of venturesome spirits were to be seen skating last Sunday on thin ics at the risk of their lives. An 'amusing incident occurred on ' one of the skating places off Manchester-road, Warrington, on this day. The owner was taking a walk mind his fields wi:en he came across two young bloods enjoying a free skate on his ice. He promptly ordered them away, but he might have shouted himself hoarse without avail. He was told - that they did not intend to come off, and when he attempted to come towards them.they swiftly glided to the opposite end,and as be was without skates his pursuit was useless. The owner was shortly after- wards seen to make his departure, and to the.great joy of the two skaters. Their victory, however, was short lived for they did not wait long before they saw him returning in company with a couple of dogs. In a trice these animals were speeding across the ice and all too quickly they bad their canine teeth securely fastened in the trousers of the hapless skaters who in their flight entered some marshy ground, and their clothing suffered severely in consequence. On being allowed to depart, they made their way home by devious ways, sadder, if not wiser men.</p>
        <p>The annual report of the Warrington Savings' Bank shows a gratifying increase in the number of depositors, ad also in the accounts due to them. This, at least speaks well for the condition of trade in the town. In the course of a short speech, the chairman of the committee of management (Mr. William Long, J.P.) urged working people to be- come more thrifty, and stated that a large amount of money was frittered away by working people. Tnere are no doubt some grounds for this statement, but still in the main, we are inclined to believe,that working people may be taken as exemplars of thrift. The labourer's wife who can, in addition to paying rent, Provide food and clothing for her husband and family on, say, twelve, fifteen, or even eighteen shillings a week, as is undoubtedly the case in many instances, and succeed in 'making both ends meet, could probably make a few wile suggestions to the Chancellor of the Exchequer. This is a kind of thrift which certainly forms a striking contrast to the wanton wastefulness which is to be seen amongst the middle and higher lasses.</p>
        <p>The committee of the Diocesan Church oc. Eng- land Temperance Society seem to be attacking the drink evil in a manly, and withal practical way. Their most recent effort is in the way of preventing an increase in the facilities for drinking, and to this end they have petitioned every county, city, and borough magistrate in the diocese of Liver pool on the question of "drinking on football grounds." They express the opinion that occasional licenses for the settle of intoxicants at some of the big football matches in Lancashire are both un• necessary and undesirable, and would lead the low- est element in the crowd to intemperance and unruly conduct. There is, nothing illusory about the reasons for this appeal to the justices,and every right-minded person will agree that the man who spends an hour and a half at a tootball match can- not be in any absolute need of stimulant or refresh- ment,. The enthusiasm attendant upon eacn team's performances should sufficiently occupy the mind of a spectator without the help of spirituous liquor. Many of the leading chit) grouilds are provided with a bar,as also are the cricket enclosures. One tendA to demoralise the public during the winter and the other carries on the process throughout the summer. N. great loss would be felt were they abolished,and magistrates would be well advised in refusing to sanction any more. Enough satire ,is lavished upon English sport and sportsmen abroad, without the latter being twitted with an inability to enjoy the national games, minus strong drink.</p>
        <p>DIETETIC TEA.</p>
