      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>TII EN A Yr, riv FR THCH SMAY</head>
        <p>TII EN A Yr, riv FR THCH SMAY</p>
        <p>months, aid to elect auditors and officers for the emu iag year. McCook was unaninaously voted to the chair- Several items of interest to the members were discussed, and although the distribution of sick parwas excep- tionally large, the secretary was able to declare a divi- dend of lac sd. per merabez,,which was considered very satisfactory. A vote of thanks te the Chairman brought the proceedings to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Tz Ruv. W. Scavoiri, Rinrcoau,</p>
        <p>The Bishop of Chester ;laid an ordination in Chester Cathedral on Sunday, when the Rev. W. Scholas of Pem- broke College,Cambridge, Wells Theological College, and of the Runcorn Parish Clergy Staff, was ordained a priest.i BETIESDA CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH, RUNCORN •</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening, at this place of Worship, another people's service was held. There was a good congreg- tion. The pastor,the Rev. J. Simpson, delivered an in- teresting address, entitled "The Story of lethlehem." The choir rendered several carols in a most appreciative manner. Miss fleatheriegtoa sang "Children's Prayers," Mr. Worrell "The Star of Bethlehem," and Mr. Gewanlock " Nazareth." The singers ably acquitted themselves. Mr. Onthwaite played with effect a solo on the euphonium, "Give us this day our daily bread." The hymns were of a Christmastide character, and the services were very greatly enjoyed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>FUNERAL OF-A ROMAN .CATHOLIC PRIEST.</head>
        <p>FUNERAL OF-A ROMAN .CATHOLIC PRIEST.</p>
        <p>_ The funeral of the Rev. Father Dupuy,S.J., who died at Ditton Hall last Tuesday, after a snort illness follow- ing an apoplectic seizure, took place on Friday. The deceased gentleman, who was 65 years of age, had only been in Ditton, where be had taken the duties of parish priest', about a month, having previously been chaplain at Wardour Castle' near Salisbury. Requiem Mass was solemnised at St. Michael's Church, Ditton Hall, the. ceremony being witnessed by a large congregation. The officiating priests were the Rev. Fathers Clarke, Donley, Collins, and North, of Widnes, and among the clergy present were the Rev, Fathers B. Vaughan, J. Hayes, J. fienny,T. Hill, R. Switt, E. Rigby, B. Huson, S.Sircum, U. ring, T. Brown, E. Martin, J. Reilly, R.Cardweil,T. Walsh, B. Bruce, and G. Taleton.Society of Jesus'; Very Rev, Dean Finegan, Appleton ; Father Rimier, Widnes; Rev. A. Walmsley, Eldon-place, Liverpowl, itc. The in- terment took place at Rainhill Roman Catholic Church, the Rev. Father Kenny, of St. Helens, officiating at the graveside. TEIS SCALDING FATALITY AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Friday morning, at Runcorn Police Court, an in- quest was held by Mr. J. E. Warsley on the body of John Patrick Roacn, aged seven months, son of Mary Roach, 3, Reath-road, who died on Wednesday from in- juries sustained by scalding, on the previous Sunday. The mother of the child ea d that about eleven o'clock on Sunday morning, Mrs. Sc ttithe baby's grandmother, was nursing it in front of the fire, when witness in going to the cuptioard knocked down a brass rod from the mantlepiece, which in falling upset a saucepan, the con- tents el which went over the child's legs. They used oil and flour, and subsequently the cnild was attended by Dr. Wright. Mrs. Scott's foot was also scalded.— Margaret Vine, a neighbour, spoke to being called in on Sunday morning, and to taking the little fellow te the doctor, who dressed his injuries.—Dr. Wright deposed to having attended deceased, who died from exhaustion following injuries sustained by being scalded. —A verdict of" accidental death" was recorded, the Coroner and jury expressing their sympathy with the bereaved mother. A juror stated that such brass rods ought to be permanently secured and made fixtures.—The Corwis. said th4t probably the inquiry might lead others to carry out MO suzgestion. Num MAWR OF CHESIMB VOLUNTRIMS.</p>
        <p>On Saturday a tacticalmight march took place in con- nection with the 2nd (Earl of Chester's) Volunteer Bat- talion Cheshire Regiment.;The weatherithough cold was fine.but no small difficulty was experienced in marching in consequence of the slippery nature of tho roads. The idea was that the five companies belonging to Chester should march on Manchester,that the Weaverliam com- pany should destroy the railway at M.ouldsworth June- tion,and then join the main company near Dunham, and that the Runcorn and Frodsham companies should try and intnrcept the latter from doing so.—There was a fair muster of the various compabies. The men from Ches- ter were under command of Major Smith; from Weavfr- hain,of filajor Thompson; and trom Runcorn and Frod- sham, of Major Harrison. On arriving at Dunham, punctual to the time appointed, • Major Harriaon des- patched cyclist scouts to Mouldsworsh Junction, to see after the Weaverham dezacbment, who found that in- stead of their detraining at that point they had gone on to Barrow. There being several roads lea ling from Mouldsworth Junction to the main road the intercepting companies were divided into throe sections, one tinder Major Harrison,:another under captain Ashton, and the third under Lieutenant Brundrit. These took charge of so many given points, and so successful was the meve- ment carried out that the Weaverbam consoasy were taken prisoners. The umpires were Colonel Ashton and Captain and Adjutant Turton. The respective companies returned to their headquarters just before midnight.</p>
        <p>NEW JUNIOR - LEAGUE" FOR RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday it was definitely announced that Mr. Speakman, president of the Runcorn Football Club, had resolved to give a valuable silver challenge cup in con n• tion wiLti the newly-firmed Runcorn Rugby Junior League, under Northern Union rules. Nine clubs have ailiated themselves with the League embracing about 150 members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Tarr or 'Boars.</head>
        <p>Tarr or 'Boars.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Police Court on Wednesday morning hefors Messrs. F. J. Norman and J. W. Lightiourn, Richard Southey of the schooner Annie Zones was charged with stealing a pair of boots valued €3.,ths pro- perty of Isaac lrvelay on the 20th December.—The pri- soner was slot to prison for seven days with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>No WORK.</head>
        <p>No WORK.</p>
        <p>Maria Morris was charged before Major Hazlehutist and Me. F. J. Norman, at the Runcorn Pu'Lica Court, an Minday, with sleeping out in Mersey-st. Runcorn, that morning. Prisoner pleaded Upton said he found the prisoner asleep on a door step in Mersey-at. early that morning. He aroustd her and as she could give no acceunt of herself he locked her up.—Morris in- terrupted and shoutti out that work was slack. (Laughter.) A policeman shouted silence in court, and the prisoner inquired what tile silence was for. The magistrates asked Superintendent °kali whether she was right in her mind, has Morris made the remark "I'm all right ; I want work." Ituperintendent Okell stated that she had lieen ginging all the morning in the cell. The bench decided to remand her until Wednesday for enquiries to be m:,de.—Ou Wednesday mornieg Morris A4B again 1 rought up before Mr. F. J. Norman (chair- man) and Mr. J. W. Lightburn.—P.O. Upton repeated the facts of the caso.—Superintondent °loll said he bad ascertained that the prisover came into the town on Wf-dnesday night last from Rhyl accompanied by a man. They got longings up to Saturday when she had a row with the man acid they separated. The prisoner was much quieter than she nas an Monday.—The magis- trates sent her for a week's•imprisoument at Knutsford gaol.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>RUNCORN TONTINE CLUB.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN TONTINE CLUB.</p>
        <p>The meeting ot the Old Bridgeaiater Arms Tontine Club was held on Thursday evening last week at the house of Mr. Garnett. The balance ebest showed that the receipts for the year were £2lB 11s. 7.1., and the expenditure £9B 14s. IL, leaving a balance of £ll9 17s. 4d. for distribution. This was divided among the 149 members, each one receiving 163. id. A pleasant evening was spent, songs being contributed by Messrs.' J, Pendlehurv, W. Woodward. Dickenson, S. Clifton. T. Price, George Banner, and J. Banks, Mr. F. Eirsley I gave a mouth organ solo. Success to the club was pro- posed by Mr. Joseph Bayley, and responded to by Mr. : Thomas Baskerville.—The b dance sheet of the Runcorn Steam-tug Tontine Club gave the receipts as £73 8. ,and the disbutsetnents £3125., leaving a balance of £42 6s. to be divided between 48 members, the dividend tieing 17s. 7d. each. The amount paid for deaths was £1.6 25., and for sickness £l3 The secretary pays thesum as the men arrive at the tonnage offiee, Top Locke. The sixth annual meetieg in connection with the St. Michael's Church Dividing Society was hid on Saturday evening in the Shaw-street school. Canon Maitland Wood, the president,occupied the chair. The balance sheet showed that the receipts amounted to £lO7 3s. 71. The pay- ments were—sick pay. £24 ; dea.ths, £2 ; management expenses. £6 ; dividend, £74 13s. 7d., viz., 75 mem- bers at 19s. 100. each ; leaving a balance of 2s. 1.10. The chairman complimented the society on such a pleas- ing stete of affairs. The following officers were elected for the ensuing year :—TreasurEr and secretary, Mr. John Rewles ; steward, Mr. Thomas Williams ; com- mittee, Messrs. George Barton, E. Hobson, N. H. Herten, R. Peacock, Charles Pownall, James Seragg, Richard K ainwright, seri.. and C. Youde. : The sixti-seith annual meeting of the Runcorn Parish Church Dividing Club and Mutual Assurance Society was held on Monday evening, under the presidency of the Rev. Canon Wood, vicar. The balance sheet showed that the income had amounted to £245 12s. 3d. There had been paid for sickness £76 10s., for deaths £ls,and for msnarernent expenses £lO 4s. This left £143 13e for dieislon among she 156 members, namely, lie. sd. each. The officers appointed were—president, Rev.. Canon Wood, vicar; trustee, Mr. Whittaker; committee, Mr. A Jordan, Mr. Fred Wilkinson, Mr. Henry Clarke, Mr. J. Bueklese Mr. H. J. Clarke, Mr. Edward Austin, Mr. Joseph Newitt, Mr. James Sherlock, and Mr. S. Boyers ;stewards, Mr. James Mort and Mr. John Yates; secretary and treasurer, Mr. John Littler. The fourth annual !sleeting of the Co-operative Empleyie Tontine Society was held in the club-room, 8, Church-strest, on Monday evening, under the presidency of Mr. W. Lacy. The dividend wlikl• amounted to 245. 94. per head, was the hest which' had yet been paid by the society. The following officers were elected for the ensuing year :—President,Mr. J. J. Shaw; secretary, Mr. W. Lacy ; treasurer, Mr. W. M. Morris; Runcorn steward, Mr. T. Clare ; and Widnes etewarrj, Mr. G. Calm. The committee were elected is?) follows :—Messrs. Elliott, Heesoan, Davies and Elliott (representing 14 oncern), and Messrs. Blins- ton, Burton and Betnev (representing Widnee). A vete of thanks was accorded to the committee of the society' for the use of the room, and a similar compliment to the Chairmar. closed the meeting. The haleness sheet showed the receiots £lOO 17s. 2d., payments fel. sick £l2 10s., and £ll 13s. 61. to be divided among 61 adult member, ar j ,nior members. The members of the L. and Railway Servants' Tore,ine met on Tuesday at Runcorn Station for the purpose of winding up She society for the past twelve</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>MYST.E RIO US DLtOWNING CASE AT RUNCORN,</head>
        <p>MYST.E RIO US DLtOWNING CASE AT RUNCORN,</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon Mr. Wonley held an investiga- tion at the Runcorn Police Court into the circumstances connected with the death of William McCarriragher, sailor, aged 56 years, a native °Marne, County Antrim, Ireland,wh.se body,was recovered from the Francis dock ef the Weston Canal on the previous afternoon, by Mr. Shaw and others.</p>
        <p>Hugh hlchiurphy, captain of the schooner Harriet Amelia, said he saw the deceased about six o'clock on the evening of the 4th inst. He did not inform him that he was going to town.. He was 58 years of age. Hugh O'Donnel, the present nia.to of the Harriet Amelia, stated that shortly betore 11 p.m. on December 4th he saw deceased in Percival-lane. Carriragher asked him to have a drink. They west into the Old Brid2e- water Arms, where they remained about ten minutes. When deceased left him he said he was going on board the schooner, which was lying in the Francis basin. He had had some drink, tut nothing.- to do him any harm. It was a rainy night but not very dark. Deceased was in company with the steward of tho vessel and another person, who had now loft the town. Witness never saw hita again, but was with the steward on the following Sunday evening, who did not say anything about him. Witness had known Carriragher about 20 years. Captain licldurphy, recalled, said he had not gems the men since. Ho know them by sight, but not their names The cook west by the name of William Jackson. One of thorn had sailed with him two months. He had every reason es believe they were respectable pereenc—By a juror: He did net believe there was a penny due to the deceased in wages. Ho gave him no money in Run- corn. No could show his wage book if desired. Deceased had no watch.</p>
        <p>Mr. Shaw, seamen's missionary, deposed that he and others bad been grappling for deceased for three days. They recovered the bodyon Friday afternoon from the Francis deck. He gave infermation to the police, and it was removed to the mortuary. P.C.-Gregory spoke to having examined the body on which were no marks of violence. It was fully clothed except the cap, and the garments were in no way dis- arranged. In the pockets were is. 70., a knife, quan- tity of string, tobacco and pipe, aryl. a paper to the captain. On the 6th inst. th:t r.-ported the man- tis be missing, and stated th,%t 0,1%4 not say whether he had gone As Liverpool or 4).)47aa.i.1ie also stated some things were missing,- ',tut Ail let know whether deceased or - n•A: bid takes tk.,- 7 rapers bad been circulated mislabel roan. fi.,. men who were said to hay: ,la deer. 3PCIc-11.: have to go the same way ;.• less hi. asa!ti hie iiries, and Mrs. Finney ga, chatas.:t6, lug respectable men. ite if Ib6 Invest. was adjourned that there - any fi ng them. Ile had seen the landlord of the oaw-. iter Arms, who said.deceased was net intc4r. Mr. Sutton, the assistant overseer, had reciiterroc., halt to inquire if deceased had any wages due a.:t to prevent the ratepayers having to pay for . The Coroner in summing up, said if any money was due to the deceased the funer:i expenses should be paid out of it. Ho reviewed the evidence and suggested that their verdict should be one of found drowned. Unfor- 'unately, they could not find the two men last seen in his company. It would have been more satisfactory if they had had their evidence as well as that of Hugh O'Donnell. There seemed to be no hope of finding them, and even if they did it was a question whether they would obtain more information. There wire no marks of violence on the body, and in his pockets was money. . A verdict of" found drowned "was recorded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>HELBY.</head>
        <p>HELBY.</p>
        <p>Efstsnv A.THLBTIC CLIIB.—In connection with the above club the annual dance was bold OR Friday night last in the National Schools. There was a large attend- ance. A programme of 21 dances was gone through and thoroughly enjoyed. Mr. McEwao and Mr. Alfred Ryder were the M.C'a. A choice selection of music was given by Messrs. Ribbert and palmer. EiRLSBY NATIONAL ScadoL—The annual prize distri- bution and concert in connection with the above school -ook place on Thursday evening last.week in the school- room. The prizes were distributed by Mrs. James White to the scholars who had attended school regularly dur- ing the past year. A concert was afterwards given when songs, dialogues, and recitations were contributed. The evening's entertainment concluded with a children' s opera. There was a large attendance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>FIIODr4tiA3I.</head>
        <p>FIIODr4tiA3I.</p>
        <p>THE " Mgsstim."—The Frodsham Parish Church was crowded on Sunday ' evening by admirers of the "Messiah" who attentively listened to a performance of that well-known master-piece. The principals were Miss Maria Burnett soprano, of the Manchester and Liverpool concerts; Miss Annie Parry, contralto, of the Provincial concerts; Mr. Benjamin lieberts, tenor, of the Pro-Cathedral Liverpool ; and Mr. Frederick Owens, bass, ef the London and Liverpeol concerts. The chorus and orchestra consisted of over 100 performers,and all the Ipriocipals w( re well applauded for their singing. The chorus sustained their part wonderfully and won the admiration of all hearers. Mr. A. H. Robit.sen, organist of St. Marys (without the wall) Chester, pre- sided at the r.rgan. whilst Mr. ft, B. Mulgroire A.R.C.O. (organist of the Frodsham Parish Church) conducted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.5.A. SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>A well-attended meeting of this society was held in Wyeliffe Church on'Sunday afternoon. The chair was occupied by Mr: D. R. Mason, who in the course of an interesting address spoke on " The Message of Christ- mas." Mr. Reader, of Liverpool, followed with the ad- dress for the afternoon. His subject was " Sincerity," and after tracing the derivation of the word, he pre. ceeded to elucidate his subject by several beautiful illus- trations of sincerity, even to martydom. The musical portion of the service was made additionally attractive by a couple of cornet solos by Mr. Pierpointof Runcorn- -The service was also taken part in by the Rev. John Yong. and Mr. William Lowe. In alluding to the Bene- volent Fund in connection with the society,the President appealed for increased assistance in order to provide Christmas cheer for some deserving members. A vote of ay npathy with Mrs. Siddall in her bereavement of her husbann, formerly a member of the society, was passed. WYCLIFFE CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH.]</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening last Wycliffe Congregational Church, Warrington, WAS filled almost to overflowing en the occasisn of the annual Chriansas choral service. Selections from the Messiah were given by soloists, chorus, and instrumentalists. The soloists vere Mrs. R. H. Pearson, Miss F. Booth, Mr. A. Griffiths, aud Mr. W. H. Wallington; instrumentalists, Messrs. R. Pisrp.oint (Runcorn), trumpet; IL H. Pearacn, A. Koowles,, grown, and E. Clare (violins), Peplow and Mat' r (viola), R. Whitfield (bass), H. 13^,nd ('ato), and W. Li, Payton (organ). Mr. Georg* Wallington conducted. The service opened with an acimirabls rendering of the Messiah overture. Mr. I. Griffitba sang tastefully the solos "Comfort ye," and "Every Valley," while Mr. W. H. Wallington achieved 'distinct success ia tile inter- pretation ef "Thus ssith the Lord," "But who may abide," and "The trumpet shall sound," more especially in the last named which was vigorously suing. The soles entrusted to Mrs. Pearson "There were shepherds," and "Come unto aim," were tastefully and intelligently rendered. Miss Booth sang " Behold ! a Virti" and "He shall feed His flock," very sweetly, at:o the chorus parts were vigorously rendered by an eriiient choir. They included "And the glory of the Lord," "0, Thou that tellest," "For unto us," " Glory to God," "All we like sheep," and " Hallelujah." There was me very hearty sing- ing, and the " Hallelujah " chorus made a fitting and grand close to the service. Hymns were interspc red with the chorus singing, &amp;c., and a short appropriate address was delivered by the Rev. John Yorsge. An offertory was taken on behalf o! the choir fundoind the service concluded with the Doxology arid Renediction. CAIRO STREET CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>The Rev.W.H.Drumm(snd preae.hed a second sermon o* the subject of "Sunday observanhe " basing his remarks en the words of St. Paul, "hot forsaking the assem- bling of yourselves together, ;as the txanner of some i.," at Cairo-street Chapel on Sunday evening laat.He pointed out that it was an undoubted fact that a great changf! ha come about of late years in the matter of attend- ance at public worship an proceeded to discuss some at the alleged reasons for it. Its conviction was that public worship was easential for tho existence of the religions life under the best conditions, and he made an earnest appeal to his hearers not to neglect th is important means of grace.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>11A LTON.</head>
        <p>11A LTON.</p>
        <p>; Sr. TuomAbl2. rIizAAITY.-011 Monday afternoon this i-c.tharitv amounting to £23 odd was distributed to Mem- ,' rim el all denominations in the pretence of 'Messrs. Sl•o§es, J. Jeacock J. Leoher, J. Pritchard, C. Birch, find ey.Woodier.members rsf vnc narish Councit. Mr. T. Wrip,nt (dist; ict councillor) rind the Rev. D. Wray were also present. There were o ger SO rtcipievts i!ad the 'tea given varied from Se. to les. each.</p>
        <p>IHLH: and J.:LAM Bi3,I3STHADS.—GARLIND. Iron- Inenger, Wa rrin gtesn.— A det RAN MRS .—See the Herald trapreveci Lifting Fire Ran ges Sole @gent, 6 ARLAND, Bridge-6trcet.—AdYt.</p>
        <p>11,1 t, )44, 1896</p>
        <p>RuNco•ta BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>1.. tortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board o Guardians was held on Tuesday at the Dutton Work- house, Mr. J. Brandreth presiding. fnere were also present—the Rev. J. S. Simpson, Messrs. S. Taylor,J.P., C.C., T. Lea and S. Yardwood, the members of the Rural District Council, and Mr. Ashton (clerk) and Mr. G. Ashton (deputy clerk).</p>
        <p>GAMES AT THE WORKHOUSE. -FACETIOUS GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The Workhouse Master reported that the games which were recommended by be Ladies'Committee and confirmed by the Board had been purchased and had coat £3 is. 2d.; is. 2d. in excess of the amount allowed. resolution was paved at the last meeting that the master prepare a statement as to the average number of children in the workhouse for the last ten years. The Master reported that the average numbe^..:l the children in the school during the last. decade wa is follows Year ,ding Mie.healmas 1887 Girls : 1887, 33 ; 1888, 33: 1869, 34 ; 1890, 27 ; 1391, 23; 1692, 27; 1393; 24; 1894,30 ; 1895, 30 ; 1896, 25. Boys: 1887, 31 : 1888. 31: 1889, 29; 1899, 25 : 1891. 23 1892, 22; 18f.i3, 23 ; 1894, 26 ; 1895, 28; 1896, 25 The above numbers in- cluded infants, and children either over age or over the requisite standard, and who were fit for industrial training. The Master assumed that an industrial trainer would be appointed for both the boys' and girls' department and that the infants would be retained in the house. There were at present 20 children in the girls' school, of which number eight were infants and 4 either over age or above standard IV. The number of children in the boys'school was 28, of which 5 were infanta and 8 were either over ago or above requiE- ite standard.—Mr. Stokes moved the adoption of the minutes, and said ke wished to add that the committee recommended that a billiard table be purchased in addi- tion to the amusements already provided. He was in- formed that a certain member had agreed to contribute £1 towards such an amusement. He thought the Board would give the small additional sum that would be needed. (Laughter.)—Mr. Yardwood seconded the adop- tion of the ininutes.—Mr. Woodward thought the com- mittee ought to Like into consideration the building of a billiard room. (Loud laughter.) The subject then dropi.ed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>SIR J. T. BRUNNER'S OFFEF/.</head>
        <p>SIR J. T. BRUNNER'S OFFEF/.</p>
        <p>Correspondence was read from Sir J.T. Brunner,M.P., stating that he had received a letter from Mr. Linaker, clerk to the Dutton School Bqard, stating that the Board Lad accepted his offer provided that the 811111 he offered will cover the cost of increasing the accommodation. The master said there were only 23 children at the present. 8 females and 15 males.—Mr.Woodward said he thought they would say no more about the matter. It now lay with the School Board and Sir John Brunner.Eie thought tbey should reply to the Board School and state that the number at present was 23,bt that provision should be made for 50 children. He proposed that the informa- tion he sent to the 'School Board.—Mr. Wallington thought the information should be given to Mr. Talbot who had asked the question last Board day.—The Clerk said Mr. Talbot asked at the last meeting that the infor- mation be supplied to the Board.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>RELIGIOUS SERVICitS IN THE HOUSE.</head>
        <p>RELIGIOUS SERVICitS IN THE HOUSE.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the Rev. A. Sayers, hon. secretary of the Frodsham and District Evangelical Free Church Council, stating that he had been instructed to forward the enclosed resolution. Resolution passed at a meeting of the Council',, Executive held in Frodskarn on Tuesday,December 15 :—" That oil., executive acting on behalf of the Council of the Evangelical Free Churches of Frodeham and district hereby appl:o3 to the Board of Guardians for peniaission to hold a religious service in the Workhouse at Dutton oe Sunday aftermons from 3 to 4 o'clock, and in the event of this request being granted guarantees to regularly provide (-trident puipit supplies."—Mr. Weir thought this would be better than letting the ininates go out to Runcorn.—Mr. Heywood asked whether Roman Catholics had the privilege of giv- ing sermons in:the House.—The Master sad they did at- bud the inruatev.—Mr. Mclntyre proposed that the re- solution of tbo Evaegelical Council be adopted. He had been making some inquiries and he foued in Northwich that the arrangement was in operation and had been ' working very harmoniously without the' least fliction. He did not see why this should not be passed.---" Ar. White seconded, and said it was very much better than for the inmases to go out. It would be hardly fit for them to go out in this weather.—Mr. Woodward said he would withdraw his motion which he moved last meet- ing and which was to be brought forward at their nest meeting that the inmates should be allowed to go to their respective churches outside the House--The motion was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>MISSMO OFFICAL.</head>
        <p>A MISSMO OFFICAL.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Henry Tiley and J. A. Hargreaves, overseers of Frodsham Lordship, wrc te to the clerk with regard to Mr. John Challoner, the asaistant overseer of Frodsharn Lordship having left the neighbourhood. They ask per- mission to engage a proper qualified wrson to put Mr. Challoner's accounts in order and to pay him out of the ratEs.—The uClerk slid he bad written to the Local Government Board and asked for instructio•qi with re- ference to the matter, and he received a reply stating that in the event of any charge being made upon the poor rats inErespect of such an examination it would rest wit tbe District auditor in the first place to deter- mine as to the legality of each charge, as the Board might be appealed to against his decision. They were not in a position to express an opinion upon their ques- tion at the present stags.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>FINANCE..</head>
        <p>FINANCE..</p>
        <p>Mr. H. E. Heywood said the out-relief for the past foitaight had been, Runcora £9O ; Fredsham £27 ;Itiud worth £22. They had a balance in hand of £3BOl 3.0 i. Bd. Several calls which were due on the 17tt November had not yet been paid. They were, alvantey Bartingten, Frodshain, Frodshlre Lordship and Whitley Superior.—Tho Clerk was instructed to write to the over- seers. This concluded the badness.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural District' Council was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday. Mr. J. Mclntyre, J.P., presided. There were ales pre- sent J. Brandreth, H. E. Heywood, S. Woodward, T. Baxter, C.C., T. Wright, T. Lewis, 9J. Weir J. Stokes, J. B. Chadwick, E Wallington, J. Spruce, J. W. B tgo- ley, W. Pickering, G. Pilling, G. Ashton (clerk), G. F. Ashton (deputy clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>TIB- PROVOSRD ISOLATION ISOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>TIB- PROVOSRD ISOLATION ISOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>Rev. A. H. Sayers, hon. sec. of the Evangelical Free IChurch Council, Frodsham and district, wrote saying that he had been instructed by the executive for tile above Council to forward the the following leselu- tion Resolution passed at a 'meeting of the Council's Executive held ic Frodshom on Tuesday, December 15. "That this Executive meeting on behalf of the Council of the Evangelical Free Churches of Frodsham and dis- trict hears with pleasure of the proposals to provide isolation hospitals for infectious diseases, and in view of the present prevalency of typhoid, diptheria, and other cases of a more or less malignant char- acter trusts that steps will immediately be taken.— A letter was road from Mr. Reginald Potts, clerk to •he Cbesbire County Council, in reference to the question of an isolation hospital. He said that if the County Coun- cil naakes any contributions towards the erection of isolation hospitals under section 21. of the Isolation Hoc- pitals Act, 1893, the same can only be paid out of the county rate.—Mr. C. B. Davies, secretary, of the Ches- hire Chamber of Agriculture, wrote explaining why the Rural Districts will be unfairly or unequally dealt with in the event of the County Council contributing out of the county rate towards ihe erection of isolation hospi- tais. The calculation based upon number of beds, cost per head, and the County rate basis 'showed that rural district would have to contribute £8,160 more to urban districts than if salli district paid the entire cost solely for its own:accommodation out ofits own rates. The Chamber of Agriculture also forwarded a copy of the proceedings of the chainber when the subject was dis- cussed.—Mr. Heywood proposed that a number of copies of the proceedings be obtained and be distributed amongst the members. He would bring the matter for- ward at the next meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>SUTTON WLTSR WORKS.</head>
        <p>SUTTON WLTSR WORKS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Talbot said the committee appointed in reference to the Sutton water supply had decided to accept the tender put in by Mr. Rowland, civil engineer,of Chester, at £9OO. —Mr. Baxter secipuded and tilt motion was carried.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>During last week., which was the 50th, of the current year, the following births and deaths were registered : Wake. Deaths. Watringtsa 38 33</p>
        <p>Total 3B</p>
        <p>The excess of birth% over de.4ths vras 3. The death rate from all causes was equal to 281 per 1,000, against 23.7 the average of the previous four weeks, and against lb'4 the corresponding week of last year. Tho death rate from the seven cnia aynaotio diseases was equal to 41 per 1,000, against 34 the average rate of the four previous woks, and against 1-8 the corresp.nding week of 1895. The ages of mortality were- - 1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-86 65 Iftr 11 10 1 5 5 SS</p>
        <p>The deaths ine.luded-3 from whooping cough, 2-from menite,s, 1 f-oin career. 2 films eld ac‘e, 10 from lung diae, rn train disease, 4 front Ability. The 2; •ing cases of infectious disease have been •‘s ng the week :—scarlet fever, 3 cases, en- try:. : term fensr, i case. T0t,3.1 eunirPr of patients in hospital on Dec. 1211,-23 MLTEon,,,LOGY.—The mean temperature was 41 3 Fah. and therefOre 2• des higher than the preceding week The maximum 1.1-'l2persture 51F., was recorded on the 11th irst., and the minimum temperature 33F., en the 12t.• inst. The Rainfall miasured 1.06 inch. The general dirf-c- -on was S.E thereat-tin of tile Anemometer denoting 40 miles. JOHN 'O. GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
