      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>GOOD TEILPLAR:4 AT LATCHFORD.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEILPLAR:4 AT LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly meeting of the Bonebill Branch, Latchford, of the 1.0.G.T. waa held in the Temper- ance Hall,on Monday evening,under the presidency of Bro. Fletcher, C.T. There was an excellent at- tendance. The programme. as is usual at this time of the year, consisted of carols, gnetings, :and ad- dresses by the members. THE (311W/141.11. SCHOOL FRIZZ SCHOLARSHIPS. At the recent examination of candidates from the various elementary schools in the borough for the two free scholarships at the Boteler Grammar School, the boys recommended for distinction are J. W. Box, Parochial Schools, Church-stre,ot, and W. Green, Heath-side Schools.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>LANTERN SERVICES.</head>
        <p>LANTERN SERVICES.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening at the Winwick-street Co- operative Hall, there was a crowded attendance at the lantern service given in connection with Cairo- atreet Church. In consonance with the season most of the views shown were illustrative of the birth and early history of Christ, and the remarks of the Rev. W. H. Drummond went to show the benefit re- sulting to the world from the birth,life,and death of Christ. The choir from Cairo-street chapel were present and sang a number of Christmas Carols. Hymns were exhibited on the screen and heartily sung by the audience. It wa announced that this was the last of these services for a few weeks. THE " MESSIAE " AT THE Y.M.C.A.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon the P.S.A. Society in con- nection with the M.C.A. held a special epen ser- vice in the Lecture Hall, when selections from "The Messiah " were rendered. ihere was a good attendance, the room being filled to excess. Mr. Kent presided. In the opening proceedings, Messrs. F. Hickman and G. Holford played the overture, after which the Chairman made a few • remarks. Mr. Harris, of Wigan, delivered an able and sound address. Miss Kent gave a nice rendering of two solos, "Star of Bethlehem" (Stephen Adams) and "Come unto me." Mr. Sherlock ably acquitted himself in the solo" For behold darkness shall cover the earth" Two solos were rendered by Miss ll.Brough, " He was despised " and "lie shall feed His flock." Mr. Adcroft followed with a goad ren- dition of two claLionet solos, " Bid me discourse" and" Rejoice greatly." A collection was then made, and realised a satisfactory sum. Ur. Hick- ml.n accompanied the soloists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>IN TER-CL1713 RACE.</head>
        <p>IN TER-CL1713 RACE.</p>
        <p>On Saturday an inter-dub race took place be- tween the Warrington Cycling Club and the Orford Harriers. The course was on the Liverpool-road, starting from the Senkey Chapel House Hotel, and about 'A miles, the cyclists, on their machines, giv- ing the runners a start of 16 minutes. After a most exciting race, the cycling club proved to be the winners by 30 points. The following is the order of coming in : WARRINGTON CYCLING CLUB._ _ORFORD HARRIERS. _</p>
        <p>B Newcombe T Ast.l.l %VCollin</p>
        <p>T Hearns J Buxton W Fenian t tandem R Price l4 W 'Litton</p>
        <p>Majority for Cyclists... 31 Afterward, tea was provided by Mr. Unsworth, of the Chapel House, and after justice had been done to it a smoker was held,seve,ral local artistes giving their services. The run was eminently successful, and there is no doubt that it will not b 3 the last contest between the two clubs. the day was an ideal one for the contest, the only drawback being the ice on tha roads, which made it a little danger- ous to go at high speed. Great credit is due to the • flieials of both clubs for the satisfactory way in which they arranged matters. .</p>
