        <head>MASONIC INSTALLATION AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>MASONIC INSTALLATION AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The annual festival in connectioo with the Lodge of Lights, No. 148, was tield on Monday afternoon, commencing at three o'clock in the Masonic Hall, Austin Chambers, Bold-st. The provincial grand secretary, Bro.Goodacre, and other provincial graind officers were amongst those present. Bro. R. H. Pearson installed Bro Joseph Lythgoe as W.M. for the ensuing sear, and the following officers were invested ;—Bro. R. H. Pearson. 1.P.M.; W.Pierpoint, S.W.; C. Davenport, J.W ; H. V. Pigot, chaplain; T. J, Lloyd. S.D.; J. Critchley, F. Howard, IG.; H. F. Greenough, treasurer; W. B. Roberts, secretary • J. Armstrong. D.C. ; W U. Robinson, Ch. R. ; R. J. Ellison, organist; J. H. GAlloway, tyler ; and J. MacGregor, S. A banquet; attended by about 60 members was held in the evening,when the follow- lowing toasts were submitted, and appropriately responded to :—" The Queen," " H.R.H. the Prince ofWls, tat.W.G.NI. the Princess of Wales, and the rest of the Royal Fornil. ," "The Right Honourable Earl Lathoni, D.G.M. Prov. G. M.. and the Prov. G Officers," "The W M.," "The Charities," "The .Initalhng Master," Officers of 148," " The W. M. rind Officers of Gilbert Greenali Lodge, 1,250," "i'he Visitors," "The Tyler's Toa.t " THE LkE M. THOMAS ROBERTS. _</p>
        <p>On Monday the funeral of the late Mr. Thomas Raberts, of the firm of Messrs. Roberts, Dale, and Cornbrook, Manchester, and formerly of War- rington,took'place at St. Paul's Church, Kereal. Mr. Roberts was a member of the Board of Manage- ment of the Manchester Royal Intirmary,and of the governing bodies of Henshavy's Blind Asylum, Cheadle Royal Asylum, an.l other charitable insti- tution. The funeral eoriege left. the deceased's re- sidence, The llollies, the car bearing the coffin be- ing followed by carriages containing the four 8008 and other relatives and friends including Mr. Prank. Roberts, Mr. Arthur Roberts, Mr. Sydney Roberts, Mr. Vernon Roberts, Mr. It. Alliott, Mr. H. Alliott Mr. Douglas Roberts,Mr. Arthur Roberts, Mr. Harold Bnwman, jun., Mr. Harold Bowman. the Rev. Percy M`Donald, the Rev. S. Pearson, Dr. Leech, Dr. Grange, Mr. J. L. Worthington, Mr. Thomas Rymer, Mr. T. H. Rymer, Mr. T.H. White, Mr.ll S. Dale, Mr. Redfern, Mr. Goodall, Mr. Johnson, and Mr. Henry Lee. The following gentlemen also attended repre- senting the Board of Management of rile Royal In- firmary :—Mr. F. Leo Schuter, Mr. Campbell Hut- ton, Mr. William Hawkins, Mr. Jftmes Whitehead, and Mr. J. W Mime: Henshaw's Blind Asylum: Mr. S. L. Helm, Mr. Alderman Grantham, Mr. W. A. Arnold, and Mr.J.R. Burne Cheadle s.jal Asylum: Mr. J. W. /declare, M.P., Alderman Xlng, and Mr. Benjamin eown. Amongst those present. at the church gere Mr. F. J. Bright (of Rochdale),Mr J.H. Lftneesh:re, "Ar. Charles E Brierley, Mr. Ingram Coke (Lorlon), Mr. G. Norbury, Mr. J. N. Cain,Mr. G. Bauese' Mr. J. S. Neill, Mr. Dancer, Mr. Thomas Hall, the Rev. A. Hall, Mr. M. Cornielle, Mr. Peter Heald, Mr. John Gill, Mr. Robert, Mr. Sankey, Mr. C. Buzley, Mr. L. Lee' Mr. SLelnterdine, Mr. Adam Murray, Mr. .J. Leigh, Mr. J. B. M'Kerrow, the Rev. H. D. Redbonane; the Rev. J. MacDougall, Mr. J. Bancroft, Mr. W. Holt, Mr. T. Stephens, and Mr. J. Kay. The service was conducted by the Rev. Percy M'Donald. SANKEY WIRE MILLS CRICKET CLUB.</p>
        <p>This club's annual meeting was held at the Sloop Hotel, Sankey Bridges On Monday evening. The election of officers was proceeded with,and resulted as follows :—Prosidelit, Mr. E. Houghton ; captain first eleven, Mr. W. Bailey ; sub-captain first eleven, Mr A. Marshall ; captain second eleven. Mr. S. Bar- low; alb-captain second eleven, Mr. G. Rutter ; treasurer, Mr. T. Simpson; secretary, My. W, Back- house ; committee, Messrs. J. Moss, S. Hobson, A. Marshall, W. Crosskill, and W. Asnip. The first eleven have played 15 matches, won 14, lost three, ad sue tie. The three matches lot were Newton, an eleven of Warrington Club, au4 Cadishead and !limn. The club won the • orarriegt‘a Leavie Championship, having played seven matches an. won seven, ecoring 14 points out of a possible four- teen. Tim second eleven have played 14 matches winning ten, and four having been lost. The fol- lowing averages ais ler the first eleven for all matches :</p>
