      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>NATION'S HEALTH..</head>
        <p>THE NATION'S HEALTH..</p>
        <p>It is becoming more and more evident wear by year to those who minutely observe the current of internal events and eanocially the health of the British nation that we are fa.t becoming a nation of Dyspeptics. Th 9 malade of climvpsia h-e; spread its effects not only over our own nation, hut it is the piewailing malady of civi- lised life. There is no disease more prevalent or more difficult to cute. It is a disease of the stomach and as- sumes a variety of different forms in parts of the body far removed from the seat nr the disease. Dyspepsia is onlY annther name for the effects of indigestion and the different forms it assumes as its effects are known by various names, according to the different semetons it presents; as dyspepsia flatulenee, heartburn, sick head- ache, pains in the stomach, palpitation of the heart, heart diseases,no!ic, dysentery. diarrhoea, 'ice. All these and even apoplexy aud paralysis and nine tenths of the cases of convulsions in children have their source in that defeltive nul.rition to which the term indigestion has been applied. Other causes are worry, anxiety,over work, emotion, stimulantc, narcotics. Many ipersons overload and over- tax the digestive organs which impairs their powers and render el them incapable nf performing their proper Nile. tions. Constant overworking of the stomach must pro- duce disease of that organ. More nutriment is furnished to the Issues of the hods- by a small quantity of food wl dieestecl, than by a large quantity half digested. The only trustworthy and nermanent remedy is Gwilym Evans's Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic, which, acting upon the ,di ,estive nrgans, strengthens them and stimulates them to healthy action, and gives tone to the whole stomach imparting new life, hope, strength and happiness. It is the most successful remedy ever dis- covered, and has never failed to gain high approval and praise whenever it has been properly and fairly tried. It contains a suitable% quantity of Quinine in each dose together with the MPiiinnial Virtues of the following well-known herbs :—Sarsaparilla. Gentain. Burdock, Saffron, Lavender, and Dandelion, combined in most happy proportions, and concentrated in a pure state, as well as being scientifically prepared to be suitable to all ages, at all seasons of the year. It is Sold in Betties, a- 2s. 91. and els. 6d. each, by all Chemists and Patent Medicine Venders. or Carriage Free by Parcels Post, direct from the Propri -tors. Beware of imitations, see the name GWILYM EVANS, on Label, Stamp, and Bottle.</p>
        <p>QUININE BITTERS M AIN.TITF,ACTII RING</p>
        <p>Co., LTD., LLANELLY, SOUTH WALES.</p>
