      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>BEFORE THE M k_GISTR ATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE M k_GISTR ATES</p>
        <p>THE NUISICNCE AT BANE QUAY STATION. On Monday morning at the Warrington Borough Police Court before the Mayor a.nri other magistrates, there was another prosecution of the loafers who are such a continual pest at Bank glay station. The defendants in this case were John Heaton, Thomas Dnrkin, and Jobs Butler, all men of had character, and described as coraermen. Thep were summoned for having on the 14th Decemner obrtructed the free passage Of Parker-st. by standing thereon.—NonA of them appeared.—P.C. Deiahay deposed that at 12 5 on the day in question he saw the three defendants standing on the footpath near the Central station, and in front of the passengers. They would insis% on cßrOirie people's luggage, and if their services were not rrvired they would stand in front of the passengers and almost push them off the footpath. Witness and a railway dd'ective watched the three de- fendants. In one instance they saw a group of young ladies who 'Found it almost impossible to pass the three men. As soon as thry saw a `policeman, added the officer, the t.s cornerrnen were off !Ike hares. They never worksd or Lttemptee to work except when in gaol and then they vere forced to do.—The maximum penalty of CP. and costs or a month was imposed in each case.</p>
        <p>Jl`7E JoinsioN's Curtis Taus HOLIDAY. an Tues Cay -warning, at the Warrington Borough Police Court,bef6:•P Mr. R. Garnett and Cauncillor Flem- ing,Jane Jn'anson, Silver-square; made her 45th appear- ance,and pieaded guilty to a charge of assaulting Edwin Pyrypawnbrolter, on the 14th De,lember.—Mr. Jenkins prosecuted and said that After the assault a warrant was issued for prisoner's arrest and she had given herself up that morning. From time to time she had been accus- tomed to pledge goods at Mr. Pr's shop, and about a quarter to 5 p m. on the day in question she called for the purpoa of pledging a huwile of clothing and a pair of hoots. he was somewhat under the influence of drink and 'lr. Pre was not inclined to advance her the money she ',anted. Prisoner than, without any cere- mony,threw the huntle at him. And also the boots, say- ing at the •:ame time that she would cut his heart out. Mr. Pr, knowing the character of the woman, went into the,h7...lk shop. Prisoner went round into Foundry- street, got into the shop, and liftorl up a chair for the purpose ol striking Mr. Pr. E. went behind the. counter ar I prisoner ruslars-1 at him, and making use o. very serillis threats, struck him several times in the chest w; It her :lists. Her vsmark then • was " I'll g to ~op gallows for you." In the str!7.E.'le she got hold of Mr. Pye by the rig. . t elbow with her t,eth and held on with such persistencc that she hit through the coat into his arm. Mr rye had had to see a D,,tor RA get it caute.risod" attd dreose . This was not the first occasion sho had assaulted fr. Pvc, ve•o had looked over it. Mr. Jenkins now press :d for the fleverelt penil of the law.—t. Pcm corroi crated, ,yintrthat prisoner rushed at him like a -moll —Prisoner sa,id : I don't remember it at all, T was drank with nor, wine.—The nhief Constable said he did no think Johnson could qn te help it when she hid drink. hut she could help L.,etting the drink,— Prisoner ;as committed to gaol for 2 months hard labour. As she wa being conveyed below she shouted sarcastic- ally at Mr. Pre, " I ',soe enj ,y your Christmas,' and disap, eared muttering threats of vengeance.</p>
