      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <p>in when she had to leave. Since then she had Leen char,- I ring and ultimately had to go to the Workhouse —:Mr. Arthur Browne, Warrington, who appeared for the defend- ant, qu 4ecl the ease of lidis and Ellis in which Sir Francis detintie in the Probate Division Court stated that the proceedings in such Cases should be taken within six months. Complainant had been receiving ss. per week through Mr. Cooke, Reith-ving Officer. He contended that she wont away on February 28th, in consequence of detahs which he (Mr. Browne) ii!d not wish to explain in open court. He suggest .d that she snould go on receiving the mo hey. There WAS no possible chance of their living together again. After some time the defendant agreed to increase the allorr- ance to 6s. a week and he gave an undertaAng for its continu- ance. Mr. Browne claimed that the summons sh mld be ills missed. The magistrates ultimately came to the c inclusion Ito Adjourn the case for a month.—Patrick Roche was sum- moned by hie wi.e Mary, for desertion —Mary Roche said she had been married to Vie d.fendant eight years. He left her last August and had not given her a Id. since. She had three children. whose Ages were six, four and two years reepeetively. Her parents hid to keep her. Tqe defendant had left her severel times before.—The defendant said he left his wife to look for wnrk. He sueceederi in gettin work in Liverpool about three weeks ago. He came to Runcorn last week to see if his wife would come to live at Liverpool and she refused. He was willing to contribute towards her main- tenance.—An order for him to allow her is. 6d. and costs was made. DRlTMX.—Frederick Arthur Johnson. was charged with being drunk ant disorderly in High-street,on Saturday night. P.C. 277. proved the case and a fine of ss. and costa was Imposed. ASLEEP lie CHARGFL—Edwin Baker. Mersey View, Warring- ton, was summoned fbr being asleep whiht in charge of a horse and cart on the 16th December, at Sutton.—P.C. 204 said on the dat • named late at night,he SAW the defendant in ch.rge of a horse and cart. Defend Int was covered up with rugs and w oslast asleep. He was sitting on the reins. P.C. Ridgway corroborated. A fine of 10s. including costs was imposed.</p>
