      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>The death of the Earl of Darnley removes one of the two survivin, peers who were in possession ,of their titles at the time of the Queen's 'Accession. Lord Da.rnley succeeded to his peerage two ye trs before that event. The one survivor now is Earl -Nelson, who is 74 years of age, and succeeded to his peerage in the same year. He is the thira in descent from the hero of Trafalg,ar. Lord Nelson left no children, but was suc- ceeded by his brother, and then by the present holder of the title, a son of Nelson's sister, who in accordance with the terms f his patent changed his name of Bolton to that of Nelson. He eniAys a p ‘nsion of £3,500 a year granted by a grateful country to Lord NAson and his two immediate successors. Thus the pension expires with the present peer.</p>
        <p>Prince and Princess Charles of Denmark left London on Saturday for Copenhagen, where the Prince will re- join his ship, his six months' leave of absence having ex- pired. The young couple dro-m from 'Marlborough House shortly before nine o'clock, accompanied be the Prince and Princess of Wales. Prince and Princess Charles, on alighting from their carriage, heartily shook hands with the suite in attendance, and then stepped with the Prince and Princess of Wales, into the special saloon attached to the train for their convenience. Here there was a most affectionate leave-t, king, the Princess and her youngest daughter being visibly affected at their first parting for any prolongeeperiod. As soon as the Partings had been said the Prince and Princess ot Wales lest the saloon, and the latter at once sought the shelter of her carriage, while the Prince remained on the plat- form to see the last of his daughter and son-in-law. There was a general raising of hats as the train moved off, and Prince and Princess Charles, who were standing at the window, responded to the salutation. The Prince and Princess of Wales drove back to Marlborough , House.</p>
        <p>The statement made by the "Daily Chronicle" that, in deferenc. tl the susceptibilities of the Sultan, the Examiner of Plays had prohibited any references in the Christmas pantomimes to the Armenian massacres was, it is now declared, founded on a misunderstanding. The " Westminster Gazette" has made inquiries and learns "on indisputable authority," that frothing has been for- bidden. All that has happened is that in the case of the pantomime at the Grand Theatre, Islington, some medi- fication of certain lines was suggested. On the sugges- tion being made the management, it is stated, chose to expunge the lines entirely instead ef altering them. All the other pantomimes, the" Gazette" believes, deal in aoin3 form or other with the Armenian question.</p>
        <p>In Japan they !awe no milk. The natives, never use it—no lowing herd is seen with the barefooted boy driv- ing them to the milk yard. There are no pastures, and even the barnyard fowl is practically unknown. Most of the animals on the Wand are left wild" in preserves. Milk is an animal product and animal food is prohibited by their religion. Curiously enough, fish is not considered animal food and is used considerably;</p>
        <p>The greatest devo‘ee was BuAlha, "The Light of Asia," "The Indian Christ." So pawerful 'was the in- fluence of this remarkable character over the human race that tn-dar it is estinrated Buddha's followers number 480;000,000.</p>
        <p>"To throw up the sponge," is borrowed froth the prize ring, it formerly being the custom, when a prize righter had been worsted. for his second to throw up the sponge used for wiping him.</p>
        <p>The 'attractions of Johannssberg seem to be irresiat- ible. In spite of" hard times "en the Rand, and tho political troubles, the steamers which leave these shoel continue to be crowd9d with emigrants tor "she gold reefed citr," while in South htfrin itself there is a steady tide of migration thither. The latest develop- ment of this Johannesburg-seeking spirit iS somewhat am sing.. The laundry question is a serious one at Cape Town,and some time ago an enterprising firm imported a number of laundrymaids from Germany. A few weeks ago thair: cOntracts exoited, and with one consent they d pstted from-the R tnd. No doubt their ,dvent will be welcoMed in a city wh re millionaires will pay almost any sum for faultless linen.</p>
        <p>Now that the excitement consequent tspen the sensa- tional discovery of Röntgen rays has sornesvhat abated, it is of interest to note the extremely good work which is being dons day by day in their application to prac- tical surgery. In a recent number of the British;, Medical Journal "Mr. Johnson, of Birkenhead, and Mr. C. Thurston Holland, of Llverpool, give particulars of two cases of the successful localisation and subsolnent removal of coins swallowed lay children. and Dr. Nathan Raw, of Dundee, reports a third case. In one of the former cases Mr. Johnson was called in to see a child two and a half years old with a doubtful history of having swallowed a halfpenny, but after a week's ob- servation no presence of a foreign body could be dis- covered. In February last the child developed acute broncho-pneumonia and other troubles supervened, and although it :I•,carne evident that a pneurno-gastric or recurrent laryngeal nerve was undergoing pressure, the cause was not discovere4 until October las who the X rays, under the guidance of Dr. Holland, ri-;?eald the long-lest hAfpeney. On the following day the coin was successfully removed by the coin catcher. alter having been embedded in the cemphague for thi etre^ months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <p>FOOTBA.LL.</p>
        <p>r 4 80, G. BUSBY 8c CO., are makers of High Class appli- k x fu.:o3 tor Football, Golf, Hockey, Lacrosse and al Sports and G.z.,iles, also for Gymnasia and Physical Lacrosse, as Illustrated Catalogc. may be obtained from local Detler s o the Sports Manufactory, 1"....,nd0n, S.B. Trade Mark G.G. B.</p>
        <p>- .i........ -....</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. TY,L6ZLEY.</p>
        <p>The above fixture was __played off at Tyldsley, on Saturday, beforettbout 1,0.4)0 spectators. A special train conveyed the piayers and spectators from Warrington. but they arrived something like half-an-haur late. On reaching the enclosure Nevins and Woodward, the two captains, had a consultattgn as to whether the ground was fit. It was very hard 'and lumpy, but the teams decided to play. Mr. J. Smith of Widnes, was the referee.</p>
        <p>The teams were :—Warrington, Heezom, full back ; Lawless,' Barber, Cross and Moores, thrsequarters ; Higginbottom and Scott, half-backs ; Taylor, Swift Roby, Barrett, Hill, Dakin, Nevins, and Jolley, forwards Tyldesley, Trow, full back ; Lawton, Parr, Meakin, and Price three-quarters; J. Berry and W. Berry, half backs; J. Woodward, J. Harrison, R. Berry, Worthington, J. Roberts, W. Roberts, Prescott, and Talker, forwards. Tyldesley won the toss and played with the wind in their favour. Hill kicked off 29 minutes late and found touch near the home 25. The home forwards got pos- session and started a dangerous dribble which Scott in- terrupted. Another similar movement put the Bongs- men en the attack and they established themselves on the line. Price was smartly tackled as he took a pass from W. Berry. Higginhottom relieved cleverly. Moores made a good mark and kicked to half way. Price next got possession and kicked up the field:into Heesom's hands. The Warrington full back stumbled and thus failed to return.Tyldesley pressed strongly again and both Berrys charged the line repeatedly only to be upended at once. A score seemed certain until Heesom smartly picked up the oval and released to the quarter flag. Taylor took a penalty kick to halfway. Another scrimmage was formed and Hig.ginbottom passed to Cross, who dodged the opposing backs and made for the line at top speed, Barber following close. Unfortunately just when he had the line at his mercy Cross got in Trow's way and los.: the ball. A timely pass to Barber would have seen a certain try. The Berm kicked hack to the Warrington half again and once more the line was in danger. Buff Berry threw far out to Lawton Moores tried to intercept the ball and then turned to tackle Lawton. He failed to hold him and the " Boner" staggered full length over the line. Tr Isv made a splendid bid for goal from the touch line. More long passing almost saw Price over, but Lawless threw him in touch. A series of scrimmages on the Warrington line gave great anxiety but at length Heesom effected a fine kick in touch. Worthington broke away fr m a pack and seemed a likely scorer. He made a ereat mistake in giving the ball a huge kick which sent it dead. Hill became prominent with a good break to the Tyldesley half, hut the advantage was lost through Scott making a bad pass and almost letting Buff Berry in. Half-time arrived with Tyldesley 1 try and W arrinetor nil. In the second half Warrington played up better after Woodward'a kick off. The home forwards gained greuad continually and the Warrington backs had a let of defensive work to do. Some good kicking jeopardised the heme line but Buff Berry relieved effectively. Heesom Eropped a formidable rush by dropping on the ball. Near the end Scott got away from a scrimmage and made a grand run to half way,where he was tackled. Had he kicked there would probably have been a score. Time arrived with the homesters leading by a try to nil.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON A. v. TYLDRSLET A.—The state of the ground on the Wilderspool enclosure on Saturday was such that play seemed improbable, but the hardy foot- ballers braved the unfavourable elements and ground in order that there might be no suspense in deciding the re- spective positions of the Warrington and Tyldesley A. teams, who, it may be stated, are running a neck and neck struggle, in the championship table. Considerable interest was added to the engagement by the fact that on the previous Saturday the Tyldesleyites had lowered the Widnes A team colours which held the premier posi- tion in the "A " competition. 'The Warrington team WaS consideratty weakened owing to several of the players being included in the first team. At the outset, Thornton and O'Brien played as half-backs, but Thorn- ton was afterwards superceded by Fairhurst. The three-quarter back division consisted of Radford, Edelsten, Isherwood, and Cooper, and Boscow occupied his old place at fullback. The visitors played with • the wind in their favour during the ' first half, but did not take full advantage of their opportunities The homesters worked the ball into their opponents twenty-five, and for an infringement in the scrimmage the visitors were 'penalised Thornton was entrusted with the kick, and landed a fine goal Stun e by this early reverse the Tvldesleyites played up with vigour. and taking advan- tage of faulty pley by ',he Warrington haly-hacks, the hornesters were called upon to 'defend their lines. A drop at goal by one of the vlsitors' three-quarterbacks ' proved abortive, the ball striking one of the uprights. Play now became very rough, and the referee had to in- terfere on several occasions with timely warnings. These, however.were unheeded, and the upshot was that a Tyldeeley forward had to be orderedoff the field before the close of the game. When play was restarted after the interval, Warrington led by three points to nil, and the game had not been restarted m .ny minutes before the bomesters increased their teats. Radford raced up the field with the ball at his toes, and eluding all op- ponents, he kicked acmes in front of the goal posts. Fair- hurst rushed up and scored under the sticks. The kick at goal by Thornton was a miserable failure. The next mnst noticeable feature of the Match was witnessed near the Warr,neton lines. Eekersley,a speedy threequarter, intercepted the play of the home halfback: end Orihhied to the home line, fie, however, kicked the ball over the line, and thus lost his opportunity to score. Up to the close of time the game now became of a rough and tumble description. What chances the home backs had of scoring were thrown away by their repeated neglect to pass to Cooper, who was kept idle for some patt of the afternoon. The result was a victory for the War- rington team by six points to nil.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE AMATEUR CUP. (FIRST BOUND). WARRINGTON V. BLACKBURN ETRURIAN '.—VICTORY FOR WARRINGTON.—On Saturday the Warrington team , eclipsed all previous efforts by beating the holders of the above cup at Blackburn. Owing to the recent frost the ' ground was somewhat hard when the following team.; turned out :—Warrington—Goal, Webster; full-backs, 1 Marshall and Broughall ; half backa, R. Connor,Linaker, and E. Pierpoint; forwards, R. Pierpoint. Jewkes, ' Derbyshire,. Selby and E. Cannor. Blackburn—Goal, F.airbrother ' • lull backs, Spink and Mercer; half backs. ' Garsden, Neville and Cooper : forwards, Butter, R. Mercer, Green, Briggs and Parker. Connor winni: g the toss chose to play up the hill, and M,rcer kick d off for the Etrurians,• who made tracks for the r sitnrs' goal, but were met by Linaker, who placed hi forwards in possession. Connor and Selhy made tracks for the Blackburn quarters, and forced a corner,but nothing came of it. Warrington by' means of good forward play kept up the pressure and ' after clever attempts-ha' Darbyshire to lower the home colours, Lirriker scored with a shot clean out of Fair- brother's reach. This reverse roused the homestere,who " wont" for Webster in ding-dong fashion. Parker z.od Briggs took the ball down the hill arid enabled the atter to eqoalizo with a" header." Marshal/ heading the man instead of the ball. Warrington were not long in asserting their supreme y, the floe play of Connor cilling forth leui Oar-tits !rim the spectators. The visiting c4rAlin centred splendidly time after time, R. Pierpoint and Jewkie m :king gr., at efforta to guide the ball into the net with their " cranium.," but failed by inches only. This was followed by Darhyshire striking the wino: net with a lightning shot. At last, however, perseverance had its reward, for from a centre of al Pierpoint's Derbyshire gave Warrington the lead, and halt time Arvin followed with the score 2-1. Playing downhill in the second half, it was expected that Warrington wonti pile n a score, hu', Snink and Mereer played a sterling game, repeatedly saving their side, and Naville a,t centre half kept !a good eye on Darhyshire, prevention any. " lightning shots:" The alackburn players tried hard to eqoalls.,their wing men inn particularly Meyer. hut B. C marir and E Pier - mint marked their men snlendidlc. an,i Webster saved marvellously a hot shot of Parkar's This was fol- lowed by the most exciting incident of the game. E Connor, getting the ball in his own ll:tartars, outran Cooper and Snink,and was only checked by Mere.er in the goal menth, but pa.ssine deftly to...Darbeshire that player hangerithe hall through. With a quarter of an hour left for play, Blackburn made an effort to win, B oughall and Mirshail boing to smother several shots. At last, however; their cif irts were rewarded, s Merrier scoring, and the whistle sounded leaving War- rington the winners by three goals to two.</p>
        <p>1-</p>
