      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <p>THE RAILWAY AGITATION. •</p>
        <p>TUREATENED TI2DU tiLl4l wira TU NORTH- EAS I ERN.</p>
        <p>The interest in the }laitw y agitation has been trans- ferred from tht.. London and North-Western to the North- Eastern Railway. Mi. Edward Hartord, secretary to the Amalgamated Sooety of Railway Servants, who has just returned to Lendco from York, the headquarters of the North-Eastern system, on Tuesday expressed himself very much dies ttisfietk'with the result of the interviews which took niece ou Moeiday between Mr Gibb, the general manager, and representatives of the men who waited upon him at the instance ,of the • Arnalgamated Society. The first conference took place at eleven o'clock in the morning, when Mr. Gob rt,ceived the seven goods guards and shouters whose names were mentioned in Mr. Harford's circler, and at two o'clock a similar depu- tation of platelayers was received. On Tuesday another deputation, of signalmen, was received, and it was un- derstood that subseepently the'passenger men, who had already had an interview with Mr. Godfrey Smith, one of the dietrict superintendents, would have a final inter- view with Mr. Gibb. Prior to Monday's interviews the deputation met Mr. Harford, and agreed to advocate the proposals adopted at the National Conference of goods guards, ,brakesmen, and shunters at Butenineham, viz. :- 1. Goods Guards anti lirakestnen.—That a week's pay be guaranteed to all goods and mineral guards, brakesmen,ana shunters who are permanently employ, and that men who have been in the service of the Com- pany twelce months shall be deemed to be permanently employed within the meaning of this clause.. 2. That ten consecutive hours, or any cart thereof, shall constitute a day's work,and an time in excess of ten hours be paid for at the rate of eight hours per day, each day to stand by itself ; Sunday duty to be paid fer at the rate ef time-an -i-halt between Saturday mid- night and Sunday midnieht, irrespective of th time of signing ors duty,and such Sondes duty shall be paid for independent of the week's work. 3. 8 unters.—That the standard number of hours for a:I shouters be eight per day ; that all time worked in excess of eight hours on any sine day be paid for at the rate of time and a cparter, and that not less than a day's wages be paid for each time called on duty. 4. That the erip system of working goods and mineral trains be abolished. Asked as to the result of the meetine, Mr. Hayford said it was altogether unsatisfactory. The general manager did not meet the men's claims or proposals fairly in any sense of the word. He avoided the main issues, and stated that t :ere was to be a deputation from the men who did not belong to the Union, and that be must hear whet they had ro say before giving a reply. "I don't know (Mr. Harford added) " where he will get such a deputation from, because most of the men on North-Eastern are members of our Society. You might posaibly fid a few place-seekers who are not members of the Union, but there are not many. To the men's request that a definite reply to the proposals should be given within a reasonable time Mr. Gibb turned a deaf ear, and stated that he hoped to be able to give a reply before the end of January. This the depu- tation reaetrded merely as an endeavour to gain time. One step in advance we made. Mr. Gibb was good enough to say that, whatever the decision might be, as he had received the deputation whose names I had given the reply weel:l be sent to me. That, at all events, is a recoenition ot the Union." Asked hi g opin—n as to the practical result of the in- terview, Mr. Fl arfere said : "It means trouble, undeuhredly. I look norm the delay as an attempt to tide over the busy sea-on, and thus take out of our hands otto of the rno4t fermidable weapons we possess. You ought to see their goods warehouse at Newcastle-on- Tyne. It is blocked fr .m floor to ceding, and perishable goods are being stored in cattle trucks, on sidings, and in every aonceivable- place. This is only one instance of many, and you eauld imaeine what would happen if I sent down word that the men were not to work over- time, which of Course I cnuld do at a minute's notice. No railway servant can be compelled to work more than his stipulated number of hours, and we could stop all overtime without any notice whatever. That is the position we are in at the present tim- , and it is tee- ressona.ble to expect us to allow such an opportunity as</p>
        <p>this to pass." Asked as to the position with regard to the London and North-Western Riiitcvay, Isir. If arford said : " That matter will rest, as I have already said, until the busy season is cv4-11 over. All the men have been reinstated in their old positions, and are working exactly the same as they wer- before the dismissals. As I have per- formed my part of the covenant, I shall of course ex- pect the Nor,b-Western directors to do theirs, and, in accordance with th" third clause of the agreement, ar- range for an interview with their men. The &amp;man Is we ave already ma le thro ,11 the Society will be put forw.srd by their own employes."</p>
        <p>THE ARMENIAN MASSACRES AND THE</p>
        <p>PANTOMIMES.</p>
        <p>The" Chronicle" says:—The lines in ene of the forth- coming pantomimes referring to the Armenian massa- cres and the Sultan to which the R ,ader of Plays took objection vr,re contained in" Cinderella," the produc- tion at the Grand at Wington. They occurred in the topical introdtiction, during a • dialogue between the Fairy Queen and a Demon, and were as follow : DEMON. O'n, go it, have your way, I'm nobody, I'm nothing. I'll turn Turk And slaughter Christians ; it's pleanant work No I won't ; I'll stand by and see it done, As free England did ; it's greater fun. - FAIRY. It's greater shame ! Ole shot from a British gun And the Great A,eassin's game was done If thus. we stand sinteas hordes are slain, Never call Us friends of the weak again. DEMON. near, hear,' mon, Gladsr ! That's very clever. Eighty-six, and talks as well as ever ! We understand thAt the Rader did not absolutely ine'st upon the linea being excised, but he called upon the mana.gem,nt to modify them, on the principle that no controversial subj-ct should be argued from one point of view. Ae he did not suggest in what way they should he modified, and the author did no see how he could trim them wittreit destroying their sense and pur- pose, it was agreed to accept what was virtually the Reader's ulcfmatum, and excise them,</p>
        <p>THE CFIILDREINT OF THE REPRIEVED WA.RRING.T9S</p>
        <p>lki lIRDERER.</p>
        <p>At- 11.anry Taylor,, police court missto9ary at W?irrkgton, had a httd interview on gr*day at Walton Prison with James Bate, the reprieved Warriington wife murderer, with special reference to the future of his children. The prisoner.' .rho eztirw.sed great thankfulness to all who. • tad worked so hard for his reprie*e, was agreeable for his twin children,Jlhn and Lizzie aged five,and hie oldest child, Mlggie,being taken Charge of by those „interesting them-telres in their welfa_ri3, but was desirtta. that the infant child should, if possible, remain in the keeping of his own parents. No in- , timation has :yet been received as to what. term 1 of imprisonment wilt be substituted for the capital sentt-nce:- ;</p>
        <p>e:UT.T.HRY and RI eotro-P, atod GOODS.—GARLAND, ffr idge-otreet, Warrington—Advt.</p>
