        <p>Teleulloae, 1,43</p>
        <p>PERFORMANCE OF "'THE MESSIAH." The Warrington Musical Society is to be congratu- lated upon the success which attended its efforts on Saturday evening last when Handers grand oratorio 1! "The Messiah" was given on a moss. adequate scale. Not oily was the society successful from a musical 1 point of view, but also, we are glad to say, from a! financial etandpoint. The crowded audience who listened to the performance on Saturday evening watt due, ne doubt, to the reduction, of the prices of admission, and the opportuneness of the performance, and encouraged by their success it is to be hoped that the officers of ' the society will see the wisdom of continuing in the step already. taken in the way of a reduced ! tariff. The platform at Parr ilall presented an im- posing appearence on Saturday evening when Mr. Cressley took his sett at the conductor's desk.Frem end to end it was filled with a very large gathering of choral morabere and a fully equipped orchestra. Judged as a whole, Saturday's performance was a distinct success, and one the condnctor (Mr. Frank H. Crossley) awl the society may reasonably feel proud ot. The members especially distinguished themselves, and we doubt whether the choral numbers of" The Meesiah " have ever previously been so well sung in Warrington as on this occa- sion. Their work was characterised by strength and rehustnees of attack, and the even balance of the parts. "For unto us a child is born "received a spl rdid interpretation. Following a subdued opening the grand outburst of voices and instru- ments on the woids " Wonderful I" "Counsellor!" was most, effective. Mention stiould also be made of the impressive choruses" Surely Ile hath borne," the conoluding portion of "AU we like sheep " and "He trusted in (lod." " Lift up your heads " was the concluding item of the first part of the pro- gramme, awl it was remarkable for the -vigour of its vocalisation and the solid manner in which the seism points of the chorus were presented. The eeief chorus in the second half was, of course i*.te well-known " Halleiejah,"to which the ensemble was grandly impressive. This chorus seldom talk to produce an effect on the mind of the hearer. Mandel himself has described his sensation %often engaged in its, composition " i did think," said " I did see all heaven open before me and the great God Ilimselt." The performance of die second &amp;was left nothing tobe de.sired,and it brought the concert to a fitting termination. The effective- nee of tbe chorus was of course immensely heightened by the splendid performance of the or- eeeeree,,, in Which Mr, R. ll.Peareon wait the leader, and also by the assissance of the organ, over which Mr. J. .R. Caldwell presided. In the fugiii overture, the tranquilly beautiful "Pastoral Symphony," and also in the more onerous airs and recitatives, the instrumentalists played with excel- s. d I lent. taste and skill. 0e 9 Tee artietes specially enfgafied -ler the solo work 1 2 6 were miss Liatiesty Currie (soprano), Mr. el aeler 1 lb 0 e or) Mice Florence (contralto), 0.83614 C '.5 i and Mr. •Arthur Barlow (bas-). In " Rejic 210 Oreuily," there was a slight straining of Miss .-.lluites .., 17 5 i_arrie's veize in the higher regieter, though in oeher 015 • 7,;1)-2cre Iler vocalisation- was neat and effective. 9 15 o 1 15 c Currie'e beet effort was "1 know that my 1 12 6 I.l,2(l«.,ertaer liveth," and •in response to applause, ,(! ; bowed her acknowledgmente. • Miss Fiorelice v Christie possesses a clear contralto voice, though 14 9 0 is 6 tie 7. very powerful, and at time-, Ole was eemew hat 0 .9 fi overpowered by the instrumentalists. She scored her greatest success in the deeply pathetic solo. 1 13 fi elle was despised," her treatment aft being deeply I. 1 . e tender and sympathetic. Mr. :Ageism Thomas, im- A,l,l.l'OTEd its the concert progrssatel, and his final selo rueou !shish break them" as given in a manner wieice cailed forth loud applause. Mr. Arthur Bb,!ioW eras in excelleet form, and, his pretation of the 5../10,4 was a i fsetty eot ere tin,i a good thosneh by oseans powerful voice,and his intelligent ; ingot the 1111..i8iC illaeed him in ti•,e audience's iv our . tee OfIKP "liby do the nations" WaF ereeted Cur-, with a tore of applauee and r. Barlow e else _seri; su-{-,,Moi in " Behold I 11 a mystery " Mid," The s4-rumpet shall sound" in which the trum- , p:-Lobligat.* was played by Mr. R. W. PierOoint, of</p>
