      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <p>THE RECENT EARTHQUAKE.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Will you allow as to ask the lielp of yeufreaders in obtaising materials for a memoir on the recent earth- quake ? My object in this memeir is to trace as accurately as possible the boundary of the area ever which the shock was felt, or the accompanying sound heard.and to draw lines on a map through all places at which the shock was if approximately the same intensity. It would be of great service to know simply the names of as many places as possible where the shock was felt, or the accompanying sound beard. Still more useful would it be to have answers to any of the question* printed hew, esvecially to these numbered 5 and 6 I shall be most glad and thankful to receive accounts from any place whatever, especially from places in the neighbourlaoed of Warrington, and I may add that no careful account, however scanty the information given, can fail to possess some value an to help in throwing light on the nature and origin of the shock. I.—Name of the place where the earthquake was observed. 2.—Time at which it was felt, if pcKisible to the nearest minute. 3.—(a) What was the nature of the shock ? (h) Did the shock consist if two distinct parts, separated by an interval of a few seconds ? (c) If so, which part was the stronger, and how long was the interval bet wean the two parts? 4.—How many seconds did the shock last, act in- cluding the accompanying sound? 6.—Was the shock strong enough (a) to make deers, windows, fire irons, crockery ttc., rattle; (b) to cause the chair, &amp;c., en which the observer was resting to be perceptibly raised or moved; (c) to make chandeliers. pictures. &amp;a., swing, or to stop dooms? 4.—(a) Was the shock accompanied. by any un- usual rumbling sound ; and,if so, what did it resemble? (6) Did the be- ginning of the sound precede, ceinede with, or follow, the beginning of the check, and by how many ascends? (c) Did the end of the sound precede, coincide with, or fellow, the and of the shock, and by how many seconds P—Yours truly, CHARLES DAVISON, Sc. D., F.G.S. Secretary of the A:idol) Association, Seismological Investigation Committee. 373, Gillett•road, Birmingham, Dec. 21.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>"LIBERATOR" RELIEF FUND.</head>
        <p>THE "LIBERATOR" RELIEF FUND.</p>
        <p>1g teat! S</p>
        <p>Sir,—By your kind permission I would again earnestly plead with your generous readers, that when they are bestowing their Christmas and New year gifts, they will net forget the victims of the" Liberator" frauds—aged people, widows and invalids—the cream of cur work- ing classes, who have all their life hag been intensely frugal, in order to provide against sickness and old age, only to find. themselves now, face to face with the Work- house. bu'.; fee the timely help of this Fund. We have 2750 caw upon our books, 1440 ef which are widows aria lone women, and 135$ are over SO years of age. No less than 50 of thorn have died during this year, their and hastened accerding to the medical certificate by the tertorittg anxiety ti which they have been subjected. We need to receive at least £2OOO during this festive season, in order that • large number of these aged and d(-,lute suferers—who ate looking to us for the usual ball-yearly greet, as a partial substitute for their lost dividend--may sot be bitterly disappointed. Pit( )us appeals are constantly claming to us. The fel- low'ing, recently received, are typical if scores :—A pocr ' widow who has 5 little children wider 14 years of age, rar,d was just about to purchase a business when the ' crash came, which lest all her haalbald left her, some .250, writes Dear sie,—lf yeu could kindly silly mei Istmuld feel so much obliged, with my five little children, and nethiug coming in but the few shillings can earn. My case is very hard. My pear children with so baits scarcely on their feat, and with ons thing or the other, I feel almost t "wilderei. If it is only a little I should be most thankful. I was loft peanilees by the Liberator Failure.—l remain, yours, Isc." An aged donsestie ser- vant also a widew, 65 5 ears of age, left destitute by the ti ass of all her savings. She first tried to let lodgings ht. fluid to give up the house because she ceuld not meet the expenses. Then she teak a situatien, but begs fee help new, t wise that has failed her, she having lost the sight of sue eve; but she writes: " The reason I left be lady was that her niece came to live with her. There was as further need of my help. I had been feel- ing very pearly for semethno. I wrote tar my. mice here to ask if she could have me for a little time. • She told we hew she was situated. She had six little ones. Her husband has been on a yacht. It being 'laid up, he is home net earning anything. I have keen trying to gat something to de, but Am told lam toe old. I was sixty- five last July and am not strong. I suffer very much with my heart, at times it is very bad. The money I , lost in the Liberator wculd have been a great help to me I new, to enable me to takes small business. Now there is only the Workhouse staring me in the face. How hard it is to bear. Shacerely thanking you, in anticipation, for your in- eertion of this appeal, and for all your past help in this effort, kindly allow met. add that Chequ:s and Postal Orders timid be crossed "The City Bank," and will be gratefully and promptly acknowledged by—Yours truly, STOCK WELL WATTS, lon. Ste. 1.11, Farriagdon-strest, London, E.C.</p>
        <p>WA.REINGTON gUSICAL SOCIETY.</p>
      </div>
