      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>AROUND THE MgDITER. RAN EA.N.</head>
        <p>AROUND THE MgDITER- . RAN EA.N.</p>
        <p>BY DORA M. Amis.</p>
        <p>IX.-POMPEII.</p>
        <p>S.S. kidnight SUR.</p>
        <p>Since leaving Marseilles we have really for the first time experienced the full witchery of the Mediter- ranean. Around our ship the great sea stretches for miles, unbroken by a single ripple and shot with chang- ing opal tints of green and blue and silver, under a sky the blue of which is tempered by a silver veil ef filmy mist. On Sunday we passed the rocky heights of Cap Corse and the barren rocks of Elba, and on Menday about midday steamed into the Bay of Naples. It is useless to attempt a description of what has se often been described already. Everyone has read glow- ing word-pictures of this matchless view, and no one who sees it is in the least prepared by his previous read- ing for what he sees. It is not merely the magnificent sweep of the bay, the queenly site of the city en her hilly base, crowned with the towers of the old fortress ef St. Elmo, the hege camical mass of Vesuvius rising abruptly from the plain en the other side of the gelf,anu the frowning ridges of the Apennines running out to the mintage frf Sorrento. It is the recollections that crowd I en *Re as cue sails along these shores where every eliff 'sr headland, every village and ravine has its story. Passing the guardian reeks of Ischia and Procida, we steam slowly past Pozzuoli, and memories of old classi- cal fable crow 4 in upon us. Not far elf is Cumae,where Tarquin the Proud bought from the sibyl those mysteri- ous books which were kept se long in the archives ef Rome. _ _ _</p>
        <p>peris, 'urn has been filled with flowers, and is cur- roundec with dainty marble statuettes. The whole gives an excellent idea of the lightuess,gaiety and grace which marked the dwelling of an opulent Roman under the Empire. We climed a high mound, which gave us the view of the whole city. The part still unexcavated is marked by an 6nOo3ure of trees. On one side were the plain, the sea. aid the purple Sorrentino mountains, on the other the great cone of Yeauvius lay bathed in golden sunlight, as placid as during the days when the people sang and feasted and went about theie daily work is ; the houses and streets beneath, in utter uneonsciousness of the doom which was about to fall upon them. We had still to sea tile theatre' which is in some res- pects the most beautiful thing in Pompeii. The covered way by which you enter reminds you of a Louisa con- cert-leall • but you are soon out in the open-air and looking down the tiers et seats, providing accommoda- tion for five thousand parsons iata the orchestra, occupied in classical plays by the chorus. In the centre of the orchestra an altar was placed. This was missing, but we saw the ter:, fillets of steps which ascended te. the stage on either side if it, land the arched entrances for eterfornters right and left. At the back of the stage is the high brick wall to which the scenery was attached and frern which the end was let down in his machine to work out the denouiment of the drains. From the top- most tier of seats we had a lovely prospect of mountain and sea, under a sky of true Italian blue, with here aid there the picturesque stone pie', so characteristic if sour:earn landscape, in the foreground. Leaving Pompeii by the Forts Marina, we visited the Museum adjoining the gate. Here are the remeias of several inhabitants found in the ruins. The csnefwe- tively small number of corpses discovered seems to thew that meet of the hahabiteato had been elide to make their escape. These who toerished were chiefly slaves or eladiaters. There ariqwo or three corpses of women. Oae girl lies on her feel), with her hand over her eyes as if to shield them freeS the ashes. Beers, the remeval of the skeletons from ejle mine liquid plaster was poured into the cavity left by the decay of the beely, is that the exact posture at the loeornent if death is pre/weed. Death seems in some rases to have released these poor creatures very quickly; one man in particular is ex- tended in a attitude ol perfect repose; but tie skeleton of a dog fiend is the ruins is twisted into hideous eon- tort' ens. Around the wile are terracotta tars and bawls, *rine* Lampe, knives and pans, and a number of ether articles of hisustheld use, all witnessing to ;the artistic taste and love and need it beauty in eve* the oenemenest walks of Pompeian life. Leaving the rains,we were rudely awakened from our dream of the past by the Strains of a Neapolitan hnedy- rarely end the whine of Neepolitan begears. It wan a thoughtful and 11011•Witat tired, though thereeehly delighted aid satisfied company that retraced its way to the Ned4:2li.: Sea. 1 NOTES ON GAAES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <p>The Bay of Baie is before us, with all its aseeciatioas of claseie luxury if nature and art, Hadrian,the greatest ot th&lt;AntoninsEmperers,sated aid werld-wearied,ended his strange and splendid life. A very different memory cennecta it.lf erith Pozzuoli, once, 'leder the name of Put.°li, the great trading port of the Mediterranean. • Here St. Paul tarried on is famous journey tei Rome, and one *an imagine with what ardour the great mis- siisnary would look out on the busy coommatce and luxury if these lovely shioree,all too be won for tbe Cress. One woadera whether, during his seven days'eojeurn, his friends tecile him to see the neighbouring amphi- theatre, the largest and best preserved in the world. Here Nero showed if his artistic gifts before the mur- muring patricians and the marvelling plebs; and here, under Diocletian, St. Januarius, the patron of Naples, was thrown to the wild beasts, which refused to read him. We was than beheaded outside the city ; and two phials containing his blood, which is said too liquefy miraculously every year on the anniversary if his mar- tyrdens,are preserved in the treasury of the cathedral. As we came into the pert we noticed the vessels hung with bunting, ta honour of the nuptials of the ?dace of Naples, which were taking place that day at Rome, and certainly if the bride be happy that the sun chine* on, there is a fair prospect before the new princees. About half past twelve the luachsen bell cAlied us below' and during that meal Mr. flooldsworth Luna explained the arrangement., for the day. We were to be pat ashes; ia the ships boats immediately after lunch, carrieges marked Midnight Su7. would be waiting on the quay to convey us to the statue fir Pompeii, and on cur return they wisuld te in attendance agaia to take us to the quay. The steams-launch would be ruining at half-past six, and from thee every half-hour up to half-paid alas, for the sonvehience of those whoo wished to dine in port., At ten we were to sail. So said, se dose. Considering , the size of the party, the programme was carried out with surprisingly little confusion It is an immense i comfort to timid folk like myself that the conductors of the expedition are gentlemen of a tranquil hoarier and a sheerful ceuaamanes. The railway lice follows the curve of the shore, and sweeps round the base if Vesuvius' between which and the city lien a fertile plain covered with gardens and orchards One could not help being struck by the con- trast between the evident abundance of the soil and the equally evident poverty of the people. The dirt, decay, and dilapidation of the worst parts ef Naples exceeded anything that we saw in Spain. It is said that in many parts of Italy the people leek back with regret to the old regime, when at least they were not ground to the dust with taxes for the purpose of maintaining a military system out of all proportion to the mounts of the country. A deep archway, the Porto Marina,leads into the dead city. The scene at first is mere curious than impressive. Narrow streets, paved with cobbles sr small flag stones, divide the ranks it cubical, one-storied, roofless houses, unbroken by any ens commanding building. Leaking in at the empty doerways, you see traces of the parti- tions between the different roams, and for see moment you could almost fancy yourself on a building let is a town sulturb,with a set of workmen's cottages in course of erection. This impression passes at once when you begin to atteed to the details if the picture. The guide steps you in front of a private house. Mersin the atrium, with the impluvium or fountain in the middle, here the niche for the panates or houtstinld gods, and at one side he private apartment of the owner. Then you pass- en into the peristyliunt or garden court, surrounded by a , eolumned arcade, and with the banqueting hall and the 1 other private apartments opening if from it. The Pita. 'ens evident'', on settling in the Mediterranean sea- , beard, took the Roman model ef dwelling-boate ad re- fined upon %substituting the slender column zp ring- ing horseshoe arch for the solid capital of '„Gt *eke with the fiat beam across, carving the reef into dedcate fretwork, and tinting the walls with rich and various arabesques. The plan of the basilica is familiar to us, being simply that of the ordinary Christian church,which in the early ages borrowed the farm of the Roman hall of justice There is a tribune at one end for the magistrates, and the building is divided into nave and aisles by a double row of columas. Opposite is the Temple of Apollo' sur- rounded by a colonnade, with an altar standing before the fight of steps that leads tie the inner shrine. A por- tion if the frieze still remains in its place. Standing hers you see the blue sky through arch after arch of the brickwork covered with yellowish ituceo, and beyond the cenfusioa et ruins the enne off Vesuvius is just visible. Then we reach the Forum, surrounded by temples. The altar in the Temple of Mercury hears on one side a lovely bas-relief of a sacrificial group, the outlines as shapely cut as if fresh from the chisel. Leaving the Forum, we pass the Temple of Jupiter. underneath which is the prison where several skeletons were discovered. Beyond is a triumphal arch, framing a floe view of the mountain. All threug,h the founda- tion of the buildings aware to have been brick,covered in some cases with marble slaiss,in others with stucco ?a hard and polished as enamel, and tinted with yellow or red. Every now and then we came upon a broken pillar with the capital delicately carved,or a curiously moulded garcoyle' giving one some idea of the prelusion of artis- tic detail which has perished in the course of time. The public baths, with the gymnasium adjoining, give one a Web idea of the refinement of civilisation to which tbeir builders had attained. There is a large central hall,with a range of seats running round the walls, which are tinted yellow, with a coloured frieze. Out of this the bath rooms open—the frigidarium with its domed roof, the Tepidarium with a vaulted roof covered with tinted has-reliefs, and a cornice supported by small carved figures, and the hot bath with its double walls, serving as flues. A walk through the principal street of shops let us in- to the daily life of the people. Here were the deep rats worn in the stone pavement by the heavy wheels of chariots, and at intervals we came upon the mounting- stones for horsemen. There were the shops of the oil- sellers, with the large oil-receptacles sunk within the counters. In acme of these wild maidenhair was grow- ing—Capelie di Venus was the guide's pretty name for it. Now a restaurant was pointed out to us, and now a bakery, with the huge brick ovens out of which the loaves had been taken which are now in the Museum, and flour-mills like gigantic coffee-mills, worked with a. Icing hand's, A large fresco of intertwisted snakes on the wall of one house that we passed indicated the abode of a physician or drug-seller, a disciple of the ged ZEiculApius. We entered several private houses belong- ing to the wealthier citizens, and all built on the same plan. Ihe House of the Faun has a curious mosaic in the atrium, reprssenting a pair of pigeons. The mosaic I found in the House of Glaucue," Cave Canes," has been removed to Naples. Another threshold mosaic gives the word Salve, or Hail, now so iamiliar to us on the door- mats of our friends. But the most interesting dwelling that we saw was one which has only recent'v been opened to the public. The mosaics are left in sills, pro- tected in some cages by gItSA. so that it is easier hero than in ether cases to realize toe original effect. The plan is exactly the same as that ef the ether house' s .the door feeding into the atrium, from which chambers epee, one of thorn decorated with large mosaics in a fine onto of preservation' and representing Hercules ,strang- ling the eerpents, this death of Orpheus, and the tragedy of Diree. There is no loftiness if feeling or dignity of conception ateut these Pompeian wa,l-paintings ; they seeeest an age given over to an elegant and cultivated Epicureanism ; but in mere technical schievement one cannot help feel'eg bow far they surpass the crude and stiff attempts of early Christian art. 1, mane- respects, indeed, the time of the elan Emperors was nearer to our own that • e of the Crusades, and we have less difficultyin eree. .e-eding men like the Younger Pliny (who saw N., • ,it ayes the destruction of. empeii, and has laU a ..,tetul account of it) than men lilse Charleernagne er Omer de Lion. There are more it tt see paintings in the roams leading out of the peristy!ium. The walls of</p>
        <p>.. ...... .., ....... ............, . in particular are ' intact Perervian red, algid croestd with bands of decoration in black .Ind yellow, represent- ing Cnoids ; in the centre of the walla are panels with tqure*.of nynithi and deitic*. The central court, or</p>
        <p>WOMEN AND WONIEN ONLY it:s roost ccolapfient to tuUy-apnr+ciate the purity, rweetfiess, and delicacy of CuTicuus. SOS, and to discnver now ua for in daily., In Oa, prepAration ~f curative washes,s,Outiots, etc. for an -05-imy cio.fing3, and excoriatina of Nkm and mucous rnembt.-411tY'i*: freenr tonsive per- suira +inn, it bas +refill. Like all others ,ft CUTICURA •• i&gt; • •lICTJECIL SOAP appeals to sverrAlArn.ond +s beyond all rao-+t effective skin purifying and beau- tifying aoap,as trell at the purest and sweeteat for toilet bath and nurecicy.</p>
