      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>RIIIFICT FOOTIIILLL.</head>
        <p>RIIIFICT FOOTIIILLL.</p>
        <p>Warringtes had rather a heavy task en !Saturday. when MI were dews for a journey to Tyldseley. The " Bongsvitan," have bees performing finely for the last month or two &amp;ad have sow wee nine successive remtches. The only appreheasion Warringtien had AM in brag badly beaten ferward. Could they but held their ewn is the front rink there was centigesee among the backs that they cold trouble the Tyldesloy line a bit. UnfortuaaZely, Houghtos Ives unable to, doe the jersey, and Scott had to take his place. Houghton's absence preyed a great lose te the team for neither ef the other two halves wore sufficiently strong to upset the brothers Berry. teiiitis jsurney of an hour's duration molted in the visiters arriving about SO Minutes late, and it was only after some discussion that the ground was declared It to play on. Movies examined it and advised his team against turning out, but they preferred to take their chance. Once the game was pine, however, it was ' plainly noticeable that the light Warriagtoniasta were I is considerable fear of the bony grouts' and their balky opposents. The backs did'at by any means otter them- selves far tackling and showed RD disposition to held the ball long.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>WARRINGTON SAYINGS BA.NK,</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON SAYINGS BA.NK,</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING.-A. SUCCESSFUL YEAR.</p>
        <p>Oa Tuesday misting, thelannual meting of trustees and managers of this Rank was held is the lank Chambers. Mr. William Long, JP., prasided,ana there were also present : The Mayor (Alderman Fairclough), Revs. J. 'Tonga, W. H. Drummond, J. J. Langhant, mews. P. Mlnks, J.P., C.C., it. Garnett, J P., J. Geddes, J.P.. H. Roberts, J.P., H. Fleming, J.P., G. Dakie, H. Millie, D. W. Pinney, John Harrison. C. Jackson Eltl!rnem, John Peers, T. Whittle, J. lisp'cuird and Thomas Tuastall TIM ANIMAL</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>Diungxxxrizse.</p>
        <p>At the Town Rail, Newton, on Saturday morning before Colonel Wood, Alice Crosby of Legh-st., Earlestown, was charged with being drunk on Friday night, and was tined 2s. 6d. and COSte.</p>
