      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <p>Tyldesley seen found that their superior weight was More than a watch for :the visiting Loam, and com- mented forcing the game. Warrington ware driven en ph their own liae, and had to withstand an incessant ' attack. The tw• Berrys time and again endeavoured t• charge thrsugh, but they were met each time with stubborn resistance. At last it appeared to occur te them that there were ether hacks waiting for a leek at the leather, se " slang it across to Lawton. Moores tackl4d, but his man got free and fell ever the liarkilscer. Mg a trydwhich was decidedly lucky. As it proved the *lily point, Moores' mistake, the only one ef the after- noon proved very costly. One effort by Cross and Bar. bar deservits praise. They made the hest run of the ethnic:ma, and how the chance was lest it 18 diffiellit to say. There manned nothing to stop them, yet the ball was lost whoa close en the line. The game certainly west in favour sr Tyldeelev, but the visitors were often dangerous, aad defended superbly. Had the ground been anything like fit to play on the result weadd pn- bahly nave been Isuk:h different.</p>
        <p>At half-back, almost more than forward the game was list far Warriagton. Scott was met up to first team play, and botrayel an occasisnal weakness ia pickiag up and passing which ribbed the Ifigit•ri of much of their attack. fliggiabottom played fairly, but was no match for the Berry.. Even when tackled these two managed to kick or pass, and had pessession far oftener than the Warrington pair. Much has been said lately about their ailfishasst, and judginz by Saturday's play alone, there is ground for the asporsion. Beth of them rushed for the line far eftener than they passed, and the thresquarters were left idle. Their present tastics de not by any means stamp them as clever backs, and their style of play will have to alter if they are going to give satisfaction to wither their committee or spet•,ta- tens.</p>
        <p>Mr. TUSSTALL presented the statement of accounts, and read the annual report which ran as fellows :—" In presenting the geaseel statenteat of accaunts of the Bank for the year ended Lich November, 1193, the Coen- mitts* of Management have the pleasure of being able to report that the pas:, year has bean probably the most euccessful in the history et the ink. The number Of transectirms was 19,716, an increase over the preceding year or "45. Neer accounts to the number of 747 have been 'veiled, ;lad the total number of depositors is now 11,373, an increase over the preceding year of 247. The amount due to depositors bee increased as less than £14.299 14s. 1141., and now stands at £321,535 18i. id. in ete!ition to which there are investments en behalf 44 deptsitors to the extent of £9,018 17e. id. 21 per cent. esnsolidated stock. Interest at the rate of 2i per cant. per annum has been added to the depositors' acceuntsite the amount of £7 B'o3 12s. sd. The assets of the Bask amount to £328,324 3s. 2d., as shown in the balaecs sheet. The surpluts, after deducting the amount due to depositors, is £4,766 4s. 6d. The National Debt Cisme neieeieners hold on behalf of the institution a sum of £323,224 12a. 10d., which is considerably in excess of the total liabilities. The committee have to report with muck regret the death of the late chairman, Major J. Fickntere, who held the office for nearly 16 years, and it will be necessary to elect a successor." The CIakIRMAIS in moving the adoption of the report and statement cf accourite said be did not know that it was necessary to say very much beyond emphasisiug , what had been said in the report as to the work done by the bank during the pest year. It bad certainly been the meet succeseful year they had known, and he was glad to say that the new year upon which the bank had entered promised to he on the same liner with a still further increase. (Hear, hear.) The comparative figures given in the report were very gratifying to every- one who was interested in promoting thrift and the care- ful habits of the people in Warrington and district. It was not so much the amotoet of money passing through the hank which proved the awfulness of the institution, but the number of accounts which had been opened. The increase during the past year had hest the largest, with one exception, that. had taken place during the last twelve years, being an increate of 945 against 314 in the previous year. Another item which was very gratifying was that the balance due to depositors had grown by fourteen or fifteen thousand pounds. The sum due to depositors was £321,500 or including as they had a right to include in that account the £9,000 duo to depeeieers from investments in consolidated stock, was £330,500. (Applause.) That he was sure was very gratityieg, and he hoped that the people of Warrington would participate still further in the advantages of the bank. They were not desirous to push their own at the expense of ether banks, but they wished to see people more thrifty. Se had been reeding the remarks of the Bishop of Manchester at the meeting of the Manchester Savings Bank, and the Bishop had a great deal to say on the habit of thrift and the independence which begat inth who denied themselves certain indulgences in order to become thrifty. Workingmen were tee apt to misuse their spare money instead of putting it by against a rainy day. They wished to stop the progress of intim- persnee in Warrington by ineulating habits of thrift amongst tht people, and inducing them to save the money spent in one form or smother en drink. Of course, there were others who spent their money foolishly. Ile thought if working eel' could be led to think of the amount of sathey that was frittered away, they would got bete the habit of saving it, they would become more self-respecting, and prepared for a rainy day, whether from accident or stoppage of work through bad times. It would be a great blessing to them to think that they had a banking account to fall twit upon. They must not forget the splendid way workingmen had carried out co-operation. They must I have large ennui invested in those societies, but still the ' Savings Bank was open to thorn as investor; of small ' rums, and they might feel certain that their money was Safe inasmuch as by the new system of inspection they had the responsibility of the Government at their backs. There WAS only one other word which he wished to speak, and that was with reference te the sad loss the bank had sustained, and also the public of Warrington, in their old friend, the late Major Pick:nem. He was a man whom they would greatly miss in their werk in . connection with that bank. Se took a deep and active interest in the work; he was kind and courteous to everyone with whom he came in contact, and a mall any community could ill afford to lose. The managing committee passed a very warm and unani- mous vote of regret and of condolence with Mrs. Pick- mere which was suitably acknowledged by her, and it , was therefore not necessary to call upon that meeting to recognise aeything further of that sere. Mr. Joie GEDDSS seconded the adoption of the report and balance sheet, and the proposition was unanimously carried,</p>
        <p>None of the threequarters on the home side were really clever. Price did Rome useful kicking, but the other three were of little use. Lawton did nothing be- yond score his lucky try, and failed to uphold his repu- tation as a sprinter. Trow, the full beek, was the best man on the field, and did his side excellent service. His sure tackling and pewerful kicking were of inestimable advantage, and he is worthy of high praise, He in too, worked manfully. Though his kicking was not quite up to farm, yet one or two eaves he made un- doubtedly prevented a score. Toe rest of the Warring- ton team, as before mentioned, played in felr of their limbs. Naturally therefore, they were not so conspicu- ous as usual.</p>
