        <p>As a result of their defeat on Saturday, it will be semi that Warrington btve lost a place. Swinton won and drew level in pcints. Having a better average,however, they pass Warringtm in the table. This makes the match down far to-day 11 the more 1111,!EEStileg% 8-vint`on should play at WaHngton, and the hornesters an te recover their positi=ni by handsomely thrashing Valentine and Co. The first meeting between the two clubs was the initial match of the season and Warrington were licked to the tune of 16 points to 3. Since then they have recovered somewhat and should give a good s.e. count of the dark Hues.</p>
        <p>Owing to the frmen state of the ground only three matches, besides V:arringtou T. Tyldesley, wets played in the Lancashire League on Saturdat. Oldham satiated themselves on Salf.rd blood, Stockport scalped St. Helens, and Br hig;:ten Rangers obtained a narrow vic- tory over Wigan. Stockport again head the list with Oldham a—point benind and a match in hand. The • present ch..mpions howe7er, are shortly entering upon a number of away matches and will probably fare rather worse. On Christmas Day they travel to Oldham.</p>
        <p>THANES TO TEP MANAGEMENT COH/S/TTIII. The Mavoa said lei had (treat pleasure in moving a vote of taanks to the Management Committee for their valued services during the past year.—Mr. EINNEY. MILL. INO ascoaded. and the vote was carried unanina misty. APPOINTMENT OF CHAIRMAN AND VICE-CHAIRMAN. The Rev..losee Yowls: said the Chairman had already beet reminding them of the less which the bask had sustained during the year by the death of Major Pick- mere which had rendered the office of chairman of the bank vacant. He (Mr. Yong.) could seders° most heartily and cordially what: had been already said. Few men in Warrington seemed to possess the confidence and esteem of his fellow townsmen more completely thau Major Pickmere. His absence front that bank was a lose which they all deeply deplored, and it became necessary that that general meeting should elect a chairman to fill his place. The committee which met prior to that meet- Mg decided to invite Mr. William Leng who had been the vice-chairman for sixteen ears and who was in every way fitted by his personal and by his business capacity to discharge the office; and to undertake it for them. It was his (Mr. Yonge's) great pleasare to move that Mr. William Long be elected chairman, and that the meeting ask Mr. Jelin Geddes to accept the position of vice-chairmart.—Mr. Dakin seconded the proposition ,and it was carried with acclamation. The CHAIRMAN in acknowledging the vote said ,person- ally he t'satiaked them very much tor the honour conferred upen him. It was an honour to represent that bank. He had just hese saying to the committee when they were kind enough to make the preposition that had been confirmed, that when he toek the office ot vice-chairman oe had no idea of occupying that position year after year. He stated then that the offices should be filled more generally by members working for the bank per- haps harder than be could do. But as it was their will that he should take the responsibilities of