      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>133. A.</head>
        <p>133. A.</p>
        <p>The usual pleasant Sunday afternoon in connec- tion with the District. Mission was held on Sunday Mr. T. Torrance presided and there was again a capital attendanee. An interesting addi ass on "Sympathy," was given by Mr. Reardon, and vocal selections were given by Mr. F.P. Clare.</p>
        <p>APPOINTM-ENT 07-SALNITAPY IMPACTOR. At the meeting ot the District Council ou Monday, Mr. 11. Brierley, (I.E. was re-appointed sanitary in- spector, at a salary of .£l5O per year. APPOINTMENT OF Tows lIALL C.A.RETAXER.</p>
        <p>On Menday afternoon the Newton Urban District Council proceeded to deal with the applications for the position of caretaker of the Eariestown Town Hell. Toe applications had been previously weeded down to eight,and out of these Dirs. Parry (widow of the late caretaker) and her nephew, Mr. J. Hol- land, of Liverpool, were jointly appointed. 000 D TEMPLADA</p>
        <p>The usual weekly 8064i011 of the Hope of Earles- town hedge if Good Templets vras held at the Co- operative Hall on Monday eirening.vrhen there was a fair attendance of members. Bro. G. Clark., P.C.T., in the absence of the C.T. presided. A num- ber of matters affecting the interests if the lodge were dealt vrith,after which the programme of the evening was proceeded with ,viz,, "Christmas carols and Christmas readings" for which Bro. G. Clarke was responsible. Under the conductorship of the Chairman, wgood selection of Christmas carols was sung by the members, interspersed with suitable readings, Bros. Parker, Newman, Clarks, Cutler and Nicholson contributing to an enjoyable evening. or EAR.LESTOWN TONTINS SOCIWIL</p>
        <p>The 1111.11ta1 general meeting of this Society was held the other evening, Mr. A. Greatholder presid- ing, when the 16th annual balance sheet NMI sub- mitted by Mr. C. Audley, secretary, which bhowed that the receipts from all sources amounted to £BB 6s. 7d., and the total disbursementsl72l2s. 3d., the sum of £56.75. 6d. having been made for sick and funeral claims. The balance for division was £l5 14s. id., 17 members receiving 6s. 7d. each, and one member is. Bd., the remaining 3s. 31 being added to the reserve fund, which brings the reserve up to £5O 3. A. Oreatholder was re- appointed chairman, Mr. Audley secretary, and Mr. J. tti, harp e, treasurer. /IDDiN4I-• OW Mn. J. W. BILIOZTA, _ . _ _</p>
        <p>On t aturday in the church of St. Mudge-a -in-them Fields, London, by Prebendary Kitt.. Mr. J. W. Emmett, the popular and greatly esteemed mana- ger of the Viaduct Works, Harlestorria, was united holy matrimony to Frances, daughter of Mr. James Fairborne, of Chelsea. The choir and altar wore profusely decorated with wilito &amp;were. Among the friends present at the ceremony were, Mr. Charles W. Itmelett,.,Mr. aid Mrs. Wilson, Mrs. Raynor, of Chelsea, Am T. r d Mrs. Wynne-Jones, New Cress Gate, Mr. and Mrs. Steidolf aid Miss Amy Taylor, Greenwich, Alderman Cook, J.P., St. Helens, and others. The bridesmaid was Mrs. Raynor and the best mu, Mr. C. W. Emmett. After the ceremony the bride aid bridegroom and their friends were. Itertain.od to an excellent luncheon in the He sl Victoria, Northumberland Avenue. Congratalabry speeches were made by Mr. Steidolf and the Rai% T. Wynne-Jones The happy pair left Victor-. Station to spend the honey- moon at Eastbourne.. THE " MESSIAH." AT BAIRLESTOWN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <p>The i:171-r-d —anian-at representatfr,q of Flanders oratario "The Messiah," was given en Tuesday ' evening in the TOWLE Hall, Esrlestown, by the Itarlestewn Musical Society. There was only a moderate audience. The principals were: Soprano, Miss Bella Rooinson ; contralto, Miss Annie Hall- wood; tenor, Mr. James Chadwick; bass, Mr. N. F. art, and the band and chorus numbered about 100. Mrs. Latimer, A. 0.0., presided at the piansi, and Mr W. Thempsen at the organ, whilst Mr. E. Leyland conducted. OE the principals Mr. Chadwick and Miss Hallvreed were undoitotedly the best,although Miss Robinson acquitted herself very creditably. The cheruses were very well rendered, the balance the voices being wed niaintained. The orchestra was hardly up to the mark, and en one or two *ces- sions appeared to save trayed eomewhat front the correct rendering. As a whole, howpver4 the per- , romance was a very good one for irrk,ocilil choir and reflects creditably upon Mr. Leylai* PROPARTT 81-La EARIALTOWN.</p>
        <p>At the Wellington iiotel,Sarlotown, on Tuesday eveniag,M.r. W. Mills brought under the banister three lots of cotte.ge property sitaate at Wargrave. There was a fair company west it, and for two lots a good price was obtained. The first two lots were sold as one,and were doe ribed as .dwelling-bou.ge, 127, Wargrave-road, Ram •town, recently in the oc- cupation of the late Mr. Joseph Sturges', the esti- slated yearly rental being .£l6 1844 and dwelling- house, 129 Wargrave-road adjoining the above in the occupation of Mr. Brindle, at a yearly rental of £l5 12s. The bidding started at .C. 350 and rapidly rose to .£466,at which price the lot was knocked down to Mr. John McDougall,of Newton. The other lot censisted ot three leasehold dwelling-houees be- ing Noe. 420, 422, and 421, with vacant laud tor one more house in Wargrave Moss, in the occupation ot Mrs. Shuttleworth, Messrs. Boid and Devanport and producing a yearly rent of .C 3.5 2s. This lot started at £220, and advanced to £3lO, at which price it</p>
        <p>was withdrawn. EARLESTOWN AND DISTRICT HOMING SOCIETY. I On Saturday evening the members and triends of the abeve society assembled at tbe Griffin Hotel, and partook of a capital repaet,provided by the host Councillor /eatty. At the annual meeting which followed,the chair was occupied by Councillor Longton, supported by Mr. 6. Swann (chairman of the society). Councillor Gilmour, Mr. J. Wightnian, secretary), and many well-known Lancashire fan- ciers. Mr. J. Vrightrnan read the annual report and financial stateioeut,which was el a gratifying char- acter, showing an increase in toe number ot mem- bers, and an equal improvement in the finances. The handsome ()tactile() was devoted to prize money to a great extant, amounting to over £2l, besides which, two handsome cups were presented to Mr. Longton for toe best averagelvelocityita young bird races, and best average .velocity tor the season 1896. A good.namber of toasts was proposed and honoured including " Tne Queen and ,ioyal Family." The " Visitors" was proposed by the Cuairman,seconded by Councillor Gi ' lmour and responded te by Mr. Jones (Liverpool), ilr.Jolly (St. Helens), Mr.iiarris (C ester), Mr. R,chardsou (Liverpool), Mr. Marsh (Seaforth), Mr. Fairhurst (Wigan), Mr. Burrows ( Warrington). The other toasts were " Prize win- 'tiers and givers,"" Host and Hostess," " Chairman," &amp;c. Tne musical arrangements were in the nands of Mr. S. Swann,who sang a c4upie of songs, con- tributions being also grven by Mr. W. Kay and Councillor Beatty, se)ections au musical glawee by Mr. lsheryvood, and whistling solos by Mr. J. Howard. CREISTMAS TREE AND SALE OF WORK AT VULCAN.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening a I;hristmas tree and Sale 1 of Work was held in tee V a,can schoolroom, with I the ob3oct of raising funds in defray the cost of ' furnishing the room in wnich the Mother's meet- ings,, counucted by Mesdamea Coningwood and Norman are held. In tile mitre of the room was placed a beautiful evergreen, and this was heavily laden with fancy and us :i al articles, and children's toys, whilst, a pretty effect was prothieed by the illumination from a number of small candles. 'there was a large attendance, and by way of opening, a class of infants attending the Vulcan school, sang , very sweetly, aev ral songs with action, under the directien of Miss 1 itltwaite. A briek easiness was ~ done in the sales, a .1\ d by half past eight, nearly all the -articles for sale on the tree. and a couple of side stalls, had disapplared. At the close Mr. Col lingwood thanked these present for weir attend- ance, and said the affair had been a far greater suc- cess, considering all the cireutustaaces, than the promoters over anticipated. Mrs. Collingwood explained the object of the sale and Christmas tree, which was to defray the- expense of furnishing the room. lite amount required was £6 10,, and she was pleased to say that the total taken hag come to £6 11,s. 2d. (Applause.) She thought this was very gorid,its the preparations , had oily been made within the last moan ; it had really been done by the mothers themselves, and they had worked splendidly. Mrs. Norman and herself had furthered these weakly meetings for the good of the mothers in the vilieste. She had 38 members on the bootzs, and they wore very sorry when that, Yttraiiier did net attend every week. It Was their intention to start something for the young women in the village, and they would be very pleased re go on with the work there, ae iOng as they received ',upper as they had dyne up to the present. _tlr. Collingwool then wi,hed them all a Merry Masi alas and dopy Ziew Year. At inter- vals during ihe sale, vocal selections were given by Mr. Collingwood, Mr. Nichols, Mial Edtvards and Bliss M. Laiteivaite.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>SIIIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>SIIIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>THE TRAFFIC ; A. RECORD WEEK.</p>
        <p>, The trade or the port of Manchecer during the week ended Friday, December 18, measured by the tonnage of sea-going vessels entered and cleared, was the largest on record. The arrivals and departures numbered 144 vesqels representing an aggregate net registered toonAge of 58,734 tons, as compared with 13.5 vessels of 47,000 la the corresponding week of last year. The largest week's traffic previously recorded was during the seven der; ended September 6, 1895,when 158 vessels ef 54,8452 ten, arrived and sailed. Toe new record for foreign traffic established a fortnight ago was also surpassed last week, when 48 vessels of 37,421 tons arrived from or sailed for foreign ports. Of the.,e,two steamers arrived from Nov York with cetten and general cargo, two from Alexan- dria. one from New Orleans, and one from Galveston, with cotta and grain ; four from the Baltic with wood pulp, timber, Ito ; two with 'hut' and produce frono Fiume and Bayonne, two with fruit from Valencia,thrte from Hamburg,two from Antwerpome from Ritterianly one from Reuen,and one from Pariscall with general car- goes, one from Huelva with pyrites, also two barquil, from Nova Scotia with deals, and one from Savannah, Georgia, with resin. Six steamers cleared for various Baltic ports, two for Ghent and Antwerp, two fer Rotterdam, and ens each fer Hamburg, Paris, Treporta7 dordeauvr, and Faroe. Three cargoes of pitch were shipped te Cette, and there were Railings to Galvestee, I Brunswick (Georgia), Bahia (via Liverpeol), and Mar Idal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>SERIOUS COLLISION ON TFIE CANAL.</head>
        <p>SERIOUS COLLISION ON TFIE CANAL.</p>
        <p>On Saturday night. about eleven o'clock, the s-tearner Leught.row, if Newcastle, beloming to Messrs. Fishery Renwick and Co., and the Princass Helena, of Glasgow. collided with considerable farce, near Latchford. the impact the Loughbrew sustained great damage, have. log holes bored in e eh side, one of witich measured Mt. The injuries being below water, she began to fill, but it. asas expected that Rencorn could be reached in safety,' It was,howeva,,fnund impossible to do this,and she was run ashore near Bandies Sluices,where she was securely fastened. She contained a very valuable cargo, some of which was consigned to Brisbane and New Zealand,. which should have been transhipped to Lqndon. rho portion la the forebeld was badly damaged, but the afterhold and the engine-room were kept free from water. The pumps if the injured steamer had been kept censtantly ia operation, and had prevented the water from leaking further headway. On Sunday barges were brought alongside,into which the cargo was discitarged as quickly as possible. Instructions wore forwarded to Mr. Gittens, Liverpool, for his centrifugal pumps, but unfortunately as the Alice was catning up. the canal during Saturday nieht she ran aground near Weaver sluiests,ani was not if until Monday. After this ' water has been pumped eat if the Loughbrow she wi'l preceed to the pontoon fir repairs. MISHAP AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At MC on Monday afternoon the Flying Whirlwind, belonging to the Alexandria Touriag Company, Liver- peelswag damaged at the lend near Runcern Bridge. Sis• was astern ef the Wenn. Lampert and Betel BA II den,which was precee.fing teLiverpool,when she struck' the side of the canal, whereby she had two holes bored in her port quarter. .9he began to ill, but was beached very quickly entirely clear of traffic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Om Botts TrusAT.-0a Friday night last week thie annual treat ta the fold folks of Ponketh was held at the Conservative Club. The invitations issued numbered 105, and the old people responded cheerfully to the call, the rocs being well filled. A substantial tea had ND prepared under the care of Mesdames Routledge, ley, Boardman, Longbow, Pownall, Johns**, Am The treat is anaually subscribed for by the leading residents.. and is most eagerly !Gelled for, and this treat was a'i suCCOMINI es say of its prelecessers. After tea wee ever, the mom was arranged and a concert wag given. The programme was varied and was composed of the following :—Miss A. Daunt violin sole, accompanied by Miss Standish; gong, I..Plumpton,.." Isle of Beauty rt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <p>gong, Mrs. Welhy, "Faithful Ever";. song, Miss MacFarlane, "Comeback to Erin ";- song, W. Mates.- " Far Me;" song, Mien Davies, "Coming thee' the Rye "1 song, Miss A. Daunt, "The Buttery"; song, J. rows .- all, "hacked in the cradle of the de sp "; song, Miss Ray, "Welcome ever welcome friends " ; Mr. Huff Mainley "Humorous sketch"; song, Miss MOIL "Gipsy's Warming"; and a canteen song by an old esilo' dier, Sergeant Tracey, which created a great deal of amusement among his hearers. Professor Grounds wail also present with hie ventriloquial entertainmeat, was a decided acquisition te the piegramme aria Dlr. Grounds thoroughly deserved the applause he rece'ved. He also gave a musical sketch "A village concert," tea a song *The Agricultural Show." Mr. J. C. Boutledge occupied the chair, and hoped they would carry away pleasant memories of the evening to last tnera for tome tinte:to come. He also stated that another treat was is view on the birthday ef Her Majesty the Queen in June next. —Mr. Kendriek accompanied the singers on the pianoferte. The following form the Managing Commits. tee : Messrs. J. C. Routledg• (chairman)* W. Longsbaer, J. Mainley, J. Jackson, P. Powuall, W. Denton, J. ft% • ter (secretary). At the clime *f the meeting presents of tea and tabacce were given te the pc--pie en leaving, and during the evening refreahmsnte were provided, indult. ing ler those who used them, pipes and tobacco. A 'but) was alto in attendance to convey those home who lived at a distance. The meeting broke up at II p.m., the old folks being loud in praise of the fare provided.</p>
      </div>
